\par\par \uc2 Personal Technology: Nicole Nguyen\par \uc2 Tech\par \b \uc2 How Period-Tracker Apps Treat Your Data, and What That Means if Roe v. Wade Is Overturned; Here's where popular services for tracking menstruation and ovulation stand on data privacy, as well as advice on other ways to shield your personal information\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Nguyen and Cordilia James \par \uc2 1,788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2022\par 04:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 You might not talk to your friends about your monthly cycle, but there's a good chance you talk to an app about it. And why not? Period-tracking apps are more convenient than using a diary, and the insights are more interesting, too.\par \par \uc2 But how much do you know about the ways apps and trackers collect, store\u8212\'20\'14and sometimes share\u8212\'20\'14your fertility and menstrual-cycle data?\par \par \uc2 The question has taken on new importance following the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-confirms-authenticity-of-leaked-draft-opinion-overruling-roe-v-wade-11651592725"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion }}}\uc2  that would overturn Roe v. Wade. Roe established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and should the court reverse its 1973 decision, about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/states-move-to-restrict-or-protect-abortion-rights-after-leak-of-draft-opinion-overturning-roe-v-wade-11651608822?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 half the states in the U.S. }}}\uc2  are likely to restrict or outright ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Phone and app data {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/your-location-data-is-being-soldoften-without-your-knowledge-1520168400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have long been shared and sold }}}\uc2  without prominent disclosure, often for advertising purposes. HIPAA, aka the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, might protect information shared between you and your healthcare provider, but it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/your-health-data-isnt-as-safe-as-you-think-11574418606"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doesn't typically apply }}}\uc2  to data you put into an app, even a health-related one. Flo Health Inc., maker of a popular period and ovulation tracker, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ftc-reaches-settlement-with-flo-health-over-fertility-tracking-app-11610568915"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 settled with the Federal Trade Commission }}}\uc2  in 2021 for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/you-give-apps-sensitive-personal-information-then-they-tell-facebook-11550851636"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sharing sensitive health data with Facebook }}}\uc2  without making the practice clear to users.\par \par \uc2 The company completed an independent privacy audit earlier this year. "We remain committed to ensuring the utmost privacy for our users and want to make it clear that Flo does not share health data with any company," a spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 In a scenario where Roe is overturned, your digital breadcrumbs\u8212\'20\'14including the kind that come from period trackers\u8212\'20\'14could be used against you in states where laws criminalize aiding in or undergoing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say legal experts.\par \par \uc2 "The importance of menstrual data is not merely speculative. It has been relevant to the government before, in investigations and restrictions," said Leah Fowler, research director at University of Houston's Health Law and Policy Institute. She cited a 2019 hearing where Missouri's state health department admitted to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kansascity.com/news/politics-government/article236773058.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 keeping a spreadsheet }}}\uc2  of Planned Parenthood \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients, which included the dates of their last menstrual period.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors have also obtained other types of digital information, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://casetext.com/case/patel-v-state-46"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 text messages }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://scholarworks.law.ubalt.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2078&context=ublr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 search histories }}}\uc2 , as evidence for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related cases.\par \par \uc2 Millions of people have downloaded period-tracking apps to become pregnant, avoid pregnancy or simply know when their period is coming. Flo\u8212\'20\'14the most popular app in the category according to app-analytics firm Data.ai\u8212\'20\'14claims 48 million monthly active users. In \uc2 Apple\uc2 's App Store current free app download rankings, it sits above the popular workplace-messaging app \uc2 Slack\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To figure out if an app can share your data, review its privacy policy for details on what measures it takes to protect customer information, whether that information is shared or sold, and how the company responds to government requests. App developers' lawyers often pack in technical jargon and edge-case scenarios that sound more ominous than they are, so we reached out to the companies to further explain the legalese. (See below.)\par \par \uc2 If you are concerned about shielding your health data from advertisers, hackers, law enforcement or all of the above, here's what to know about how some of the most popular period trackers\u8212\'20\'14as well as other apps\u8212\'20\'14handle your information.\par \par \uc2 Mind the App\par \par \uc2 Different types of data, including information that can be subpoenaed from period trackers, can create an extremely detailed profile of you when combined. Prof. Fowler says she thinks it is likely that user data will have greater importance if more places criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While period trackers collect and store health data, there aren't typically special protections governing that information, said Prof. Fowler. Apps can use your data how they choose as outlined in their privacy policies, she said, adding that ideally the data would be stored on your devices\u8212\'20\'14rather than in the cloud\u8212\'20\'14and not be subject to third-party tracking.\par \par \uc2 When vetting the policy of your preferred app, here are a few keywords to search for (Command or Control + F will come in handy).\par \par \uc2 * "Encrypt": Your data should be encrypted\u8212\'20\'14that is, scrambled into an incoherent string of code when it's traveling to the server or stored on the server\u8212\'20\'14to prevent hackers from accessing it. Often, companies retain keys to unscramble the code.\par \par \uc2 An even more secure form of protection is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/encryption-apps-apple-imessage-meta-whatsapp-telegram-11654293068"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 end-to-end encryption }}}\uc2 . Not even the company can see your data, so it doesn't have anything useful to share with marketers or law enforcement in the case of a subpoena, court order or search warrant. Only your device has the keys.\par \par \uc2 Of the period trackers whose data practices we reviewed, only Apple's Health app offers end-to-end encryption.\par \par \uc2 * "Share" or "Sell": If your data could be shared or sold to advertisers or other third parties, you may want to reconsider using the app.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to period trackers, Glow says it can share personal information for advertising purposes. \uc2 Apple\uc2  says data from its Health app can't be shared or sold because it is encrypted end-to-end. The makers of Flo and \uc2 Fitbit\uc2  say they don't sell personal data, but may share some anonymized data with marketing firms. The makers of Clue and Natural Cycles have similar policies, but allow their users to opt out.\par \par \uc2 * "Requests": Legal experts say companies should notify users when a court or government agency asks them for data, ideally before complying with the request. Larger companies such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://support.google.com/transparencyreport/answer/9713961"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Google }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.apple.com/privacy/government-information-requests/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Apple }}}\uc2  publish transparency reports disclosing the scope of incoming requests.\par \par \uc2 All of these apps' privacy policies say they will comply with legal requests. Flo, \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Fitbit\uc2  have policies for notifying users when their data is requested.\par \par \uc2 Be wary of free apps, which often make money by selling ads or user data. (Also note any apps that ask for your location but don't provide any visible use for the information, as it can be used in ad targeting.) Using a paid app isn't a guarantee that your data is private. While Glow Inc. offers paid upgrades to its free app, its policy states that the company can still share or sell your data. Glow didn't respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Also bear in mind: Emails, messages and web searches have historically been the primary data used to build cases against people seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, said Cynthia Conti-Cook, tech fellow at the \uc2 Ford Foundation\uc2 , a nonpartisan human-rights nonprofit.\par \par \uc2 "Search history and text messages actually reveal what someone was thinking, curious about, and searching for as opposed to what can be extrapolated either through locations visited or period details entered or not entered," said Ms. Conti-Cook, author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://scholarworks.law.ubalt.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2078&context=ublr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a widely cited study }}}\uc2  on the role of digital forensics in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related prosecutions.You could take your tracking off digital devices altogether. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://worldofprintables.com/period-tracker/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Free printable templates }}}\uc2  let you keep records the old-school way, as do {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthood.org/uploads/filer_public/2a/3d/2a3d3a12-4cd2-4803-a70a-5de3ab66909f/trackingyourfert_june2015_r2_1_2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fertility awareness charts }}}\uc2  for determining ovulation. After all, pen and paper can be counted on to not leak data. Period.\par \par \uc2 The Apps and Data Privacy\par \par \uc2 Here's where the most-downloaded fertility apps stand on data privacy.\par \par \uc2 Flo\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://flo.health/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Flo }}}\uc2 , the most downloaded app in the category, is free to use for period and ovulation predictions and symptom tracking. A premium subscription comes with additional features, such as insight into your likelihood of getting pregnant on a given day.\par \par \uc2 * Data security: Encrypted but not end-to-end.\par \par \uc2 * Data sharing: Doesn't sell user information to advertisers or data brokers, though it may share limited data, including age group and general location, with marketing platforms.\par \par \uc2 * Law-enforcement policy: Complies with valid requests and notifies affected users, unless where legally prohibited.\par \par \uc2 Apple Health\par \par \uc2 The Health app is pre-installed on iPhones, and offers period and fertility window predictions. The cycle tracking is buried behind a few taps and menus.\par \par \uc2 * Data security: End-to-end encrypted.\par \par \uc2 * Data sharing: May share data with third parties, but doesn't have health data to share.\par \par \uc2 * Law-enforcement policy: Complies with valid requests, but doesn't have data to share.\par \par \uc2 Clue\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://helloclue.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Clue }}}\uc2  can track periods, ovulation, cycle symptoms and pregnancy. The company is launching a separate birth-control app cleared by the \uc2 U.S. Food and Drug Administration\uc2  later this year.\par \par \uc2 * Data security: Encrypted but not end-to-end.\par \par \uc2 * Data sharing: Doesn't sell user data to advertisers or data brokers, though it may share anonymized data with researchers and non-health data with advertising networks unless users opt out.\par \par \uc2 * Law-enforcement policy: Doesn't have a policy for governmental requests specifically; a spokeswoman said Clue "will not disclose user data" due to European privacy law.\par \par \uc2 Fitbit\par \par \uc2 Fitbit\uc2 's fitness app can track periods, and offers period and fertile-window prediction, as well as cycle trends.\par \par \uc2 * Data security: Encrypted but not end-to-end.\par \par \uc2 * Data sharing: Doesn't sell users' personal information to advertisers, though it may share data with firms hired to market \uc2 Fitbit\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Law-enforcement policy: Complies with valid requests and notifies affected users, unless where legally prohibited.\par \par \uc2 Glow\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://glowing.com/apps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Glow }}}\uc2  is a fertility calendar and period tracker. The company also has a period and sex-specific app called Eve, as well as a pregnancy-tracking app called Nurture.\par \par \uc2 * Data security: Implements "technical safeguards" around data but doesn't mention encryption.\par \par \uc2 * Data sharing; May use personal information for marketing and targeted advertising, and share data with advertising partners.\par \par \uc2 * Law-enforcement policy: Complies with valid requests, but its policy doesn't mention notifying affected users.\par \par \uc2 Natural Cycles\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.naturalcycles.com/how-does-natural-cycles-work"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Natural Cycles }}}\uc2 , the first birth-control app cleared by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , has two million registered users. Users must regularly take their temperature to use the app to avoid pregnancy. It costs $100 a year or $13 a month and has no free version.\par \par \uc2 * Data security: Encrypted but not end-to-end.\par \par \uc2 * Data sharing: Doesn't share or sell personal health data, though other information can be shared to market the app, unless the user opts out.\par \par \uc2 * Law-enforcement policy: Complies with valid requests; a spokeswoman said the company would notify affected users and has never received a legal request for information.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14For more WSJ Technology analysis, reviews, advice and headlines, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/newsletters?sub=55&mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sign up for our weekly newsletter }}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Write to Nicole Nguyen at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:nicole.nguyen@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nicole.nguyen@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Cordilia James at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:cordilia.james@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cordilia.james@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-period-tracker-apps-treat-your-data-and-what-that-means-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11655561595"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 How Period-Tracker Apps Treat Your Data, and What That Means if Roe v. Wade Is Overturned }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220619ei6j000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
