\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 States Prepare to Quickly Implement  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  'Trigger' Laws; The regulations, designed to go into effect if Supreme Court overturns Roe v. Wade, would ban most abortions in 13 states\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas \par \uc2 1,059 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2022\par 13:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 States with so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trigger laws are dusting off the regulations in preparation for the possible overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers passed these \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on the condition they would go into effect if the Supreme Court rescinded the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many of the laws, which would ban most abortions in those states, have been on the books for years\u8212\'20\'14and in some cases, more than a decade. But they gained renewed interest after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 draft Supreme Court opinion }}}\uc2 , leaked in May, indicated the court may be preparing to reverse Roe, the 1973 decision that established the constitutional right to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 State lawmakers, attorneys general and governors are now considering the regulations and taking steps to ensure they take effect, should they need to. And, in some instances, weighing increased criminal penalties or new exceptions.\par \par \uc2 In Missouri, a state senator introduced a resolution recognizing the high court's potential decision, a first step in positioning a trigger law to be enforced there once the decision is final.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi Gov. \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , a Republican, said the state's trigger law\u8212\'20\'14which passed in 2007 and would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in cases of medical emergency or instances of rape reported to law enforcement\u8212\'20\'14would supersede other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Mississippi's 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-mississippi-abortion-case-at-the-supreme-court-what-you-should-know-11621337351"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 center of the case currently before }}}\uc2  the Supreme Court, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 The trigger laws, while similar in their end goal, would go into effect at different times through varying state-level actions. Laws in Kentucky, Louisiana and South Dakota would be enforceable immediately should Roe be overturned.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, Idaho and Tennessee, trigger laws would take effect 30 days after a Supreme Court decision eliminating the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And in Arkansas, Mississippi, North Dakota, Missouri, Oklahoma, Wyoming and Utah, state lawmakers, attorneys general or governors would have to certify the high court's decision to allow the bans to take effect. Penalties for providers who violate these laws also differ, including two to five years in prison in Idaho and up to 10 years in prison in Arkansas, highlighting the complicated legal landscape that may lie ahead.\par \par \uc2 "We'll see variation across all states, but also variation among states that have already solidly verified what their policy preference is going to be," Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, interim dean of the Beasley School of Law at \uc2 Temple University\uc2  and an expert on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive-health\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, said of a possible post-Roe America. "Rather than thinking of a new era of workability, I think you're going to see an era of interstate conflict and of perplexity."\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma's 2021 trigger law would permit the state to enforce existing criminal statutes that criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the Supreme Court overrule Roe and the 1992 decision that largely reaffirmed it, Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pennsylvania v. Casey.\par \par \uc2 They tweaked the law in April in light of the high court's oral arguments in the Mississippi case in December. State lawmakers interpreted the arguments as raising the possibility the Supreme Court could uphold the 15-week ban but not overturn its past \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights precedents entirely, said Oklahoma Senate President Pro Tempore Gregory Treat. Now, the trigger law could take effect if the Supreme Court either overrules the decisions "in whole or in part."\par \par \uc2 "We're trying to keep on top of that," Mr. Treat, a Republican, said of the trigger law. "We wanted to make sure we were covered either way the court made a ruling."\par \par \uc2 A near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/oklahoma-governor-signs-abortion-ban-11653529937"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is already in effect }}}\uc2  in Oklahoma. The May law made nearly all abortions illegal, deputized private citizens with enforcing it, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/oklahomas-new-abortion-ban-leaves-clinics-reeling-11653610467"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 left clinics reeling }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Louisiana lawmakers debated whether to include exceptions for rape and incest in a bill streamlining the state's existing trigger law and other similar legislation earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 "I know that there are changes that are coming," said Rep. Cedric Glover, a Democrat who proposed the exceptions, of the coming Supreme Court decision. "At the very least let us give those who have been raped or the victims of incest access to a medically safe and legal option."\par \par \uc2 The state House didn't adopt the exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Arkansas Gov. Asa Hutchinson has said he hopes exceptions for rape and incest would be added to the state's trigger law, which he signed in 2019. The law, as passed, would ban all abortions with one exception\u8212\'20\'14to save the life of the woman.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do you expect the Supreme Court to narrow or overturn Roe v. Wade? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Shealyn Sowers, a spokeswoman for the governor, said late last month he wouldn't call a special session that could address those questions until after a Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 In South Dakota, lawmakers are starting to consider what kind of antiabortion laws to bring forward should the governor call a special session there. GOP Rep. \uc2 Jon Hansen\uc2 , speaker pro tempore of the state House of Representatives, hopes to ratchet up criminal penalties in the state's trigger law, increasing them from two years in prison to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , a Republican, has said she would call a special session on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions should the Supreme Court's decision fall in line with the leaked draft.\par \par \uc2 "People are starting to wrap their minds around what the possibilities are if Roe is overturned," Mr. Hansen said.\par \par \uc2 In May, Missouri state Sen. Justin Brown introduced a resolution to recognize the Supreme Court's decision should it overturn Roe. Such legislative action, or a certification of the ruling by the governor or attorney general, is needed to implement the state's trigger law, which bans abortions in the state except in cases of medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 The legislative session ended before Mr. Brown's resolution received full consideration.\par \par \uc2 "Who would've thought three years ago that this would be happening?" Mr. Brown, a Republican, said in an interview. "But that's why we put that trigger in there."\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/states-prepare-to-quickly-implement-abortion-trigger-laws-11655385028"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 States Prepare to Quickly Implement \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  'Trigger' Laws }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220616ei6g002xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
