\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Yes, \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Was Pro-Life; She and Elizabeth Cady Stanton railed against  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in the pages of their paper.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Colleen Kelly Spellecy and Eric Anthony \par \uc2 828 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2022\par 22:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Early feminist icons Elizabeth Cady Stanton and \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  changed forever the role of women in American society. In the 19th century they tirelessly promoted public education on behalf of women's equality and demanded that women be given the right to vote. But contrary to recent claims by advocacy groups, they were not in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After a leaked Supreme Court draft decision appearing to overturn Roe v. Wade circulated in May, the nonprofit National \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Museum and House in Rochester, N.Y., {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://susanb.org/susan-b-anthony-vs-the-list/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused}}}\uc2  the Susan B. Anthony List, a pro-life political action committee, of misappropriating the famous suffragist's name. "To suggest that \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  would support government intervention in a woman's decision about a pregnancy is abhorrent," museum president \uc2 Deborah L. Hughes\uc2  wrote in a post on the organization's website.\par \par \uc2 The Elizabeth Cady Stanton Trust similarly claims that its namesake would have supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocacy in the 21st century. The group has filed lawsuits in Michigan, New York and Rhode Island, asking courts, according to a press release, "to protect Roe v. Wade from being overturned, and firmly establish the Equal Rights Amendment . . . in the United States Constitution."\par \par \uc2 In fact, it's the pro-choice groups that have it wrong. During their lifetimes, both women vociferously condemned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They certainly wouldn't have embraced the use of their names to promote what they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.susanbanthonybirthplace.com/_files/ugd/75daee_9a02afcb92b4440e991b29e4d1f39985.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 termed}}}\uc2  "foeticide."\par \par \uc2 Stanton was editor of the Revolution, a newspaper owned and published by Anthony. The publication stood out, even among other pro-suffrage newspapers, for its steadfast refusal to be polite. Stanton wrote editorials and published articles on subjects such as rape and prostitution that no one else would touch.\par \par \uc2 Next to winning the vote for women, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was one of the most discussed topics in the Revolution's pages. The practice was unanimously reviled and rebuked in more than 100 editorials, letters to the editor and articles. While mainstream newspapers of the time often reported on deaths stemming from abortions gone wrong and the arrests of those performing illegal abortions, the Revolution focused instead on the brutality of removing a fetus from a mother's womb. The paper's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.susanbanthonybirthplace.com/_files/ugd/75daee_797db11eda744581b801f753a693344f.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 editorial voice called \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  "infanticide" and "child murder."\par \par \uc2 In the Revolution's January 1868 inaugural edition, the editors declared that no advertisements for "quack or immoral medicines" would run in their pages. That this policy referred specifically to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs was explained in 20 editorials in subsequent issues, including an Oct. 19, 1868, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.susanbanthonybirthplace.com/_files/ugd/b63d74_449da1d79ac74b3ca4c1e20192ee4cf0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 item}}}\uc2  headlined "Quacks and their Drugs and Deeds." The editors railed against those "who sell or distribute drugs and nostrums to prevent conception or procure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." This "infamous class," the paper notes, "make a living by the slaughter of their own race."\par \par \uc2 Stanton and Anthony shared a revulsion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Stanton put a whole new spin on the idea of equality of the sexes\u8212\'20\'14she wanted to elevate men to the level of women. "We are, as a sex, infinitely superior to men, and if we were free and developed, healthy in body and mind, as we should be under natural conditions, our motherhood would be our glory," she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/books/edition/Elizabeth_Cady_Stanton/CIsEAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=We are, as a sex, infinitely superior to men,and if we were free... Elizabeth Cady Stanton&pg=PA270&printsec=frontcover"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2 . "That function gives women such wisdom and power as no male can possess."\par \par \uc2 The suffragist movement grew out of the abolitionist crusade. Writers like Anthony and Stanton were well-skilled in professing the evils of slavery. They saw a connection between social acceptance of slavery and the tendency to believe that wives and children were the property of their husbands. "When we consider that women are treated as property, it is degrading to women that we should treat our children as property to be disposed of as we see fit," Stanton {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ecswc.org/later-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2 in 1873.\par \par \uc2 Early maternal medicine relied on "quickening"\u8212\'20\'14when a woman can feel her baby moving\u8212\'20\'14to determine pregnancy and fetal development. But Stanton and Anthony reprinted lectures by Anna Densmore French on the science of embryology. "No greater error exists," French insisted, than to interfere with "the embryo child" whose life exists prior to "physical movements conscious to the mother."\par \par \uc2 Besides educating women about pregnancy, suffragists believed if women were allowed to vote and earn their own money, single motherhood would become more socially accepted. The "murder of children, either before or after birth," as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.susanbanthonybirthplace.com/_files/ugd/75daee_797db11eda744581b801f753a693344f.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Revolution put it}}}\uc2  on Feb. 5, 1868, would decrease.\par \par \uc2 Modern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists are wrong to enlist Elizabeth Cady Stanton and \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  in their cause. Both women were firmly opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as their extensive writing and publishing on the subject confirms. Arguments to the contrary do a disservice to women\u8212\'20\'14and to women's history.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Spellecy and Mr. Anthony are board members of the Susan B. Anthony Birthplace Museum in Adams, Mass.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/susan-b-anthony-was-pro-life-elizabeth-cady-stanton-roe-abortion-dobbs-decision-11655151459"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Yes, Susan B. Anthony Was Pro-Life}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220613ei6d006bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
