\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Targeting the Supreme Court; Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has been sitting on a bill to protect the Justices' families.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 743 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2022\par 23:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The arrest early Wednesday of an armed man outside Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 's Maryland home is a depressing sign of the fanatical times. Violence in the name of politics now threatens the judiciary, and Washington's remaining adults have an obligation to act with dispatch to protect the Justices, their families and the Court as an institution.\par \par \uc2 Californian Nicholas John Roske, 26 years old, was arrested at night dressed in black. He allegedly had a suitcase, backpack, a Glock 17 with two magazines and ammunition, pepper spray, a tactical knife, hammer, screwdriver, crow bar, zip ties and duct tape.\par \par \uc2 According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/06/08/kavanaugh-threat-arrest-justice/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Washington Post}}}\uc2 , Mr. Roske told police he "was upset over the leaked draft of an opinion that would overturn the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Roe v. Wade. The man allegedly said he came to kill Justice Kavanaugh "thinking it would give his life purpose." He has been charged with attempted murder.\par \par \uc2 We warned after the leak of Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 's draft opinion that a fanatic could attempt a violent act against one of the Justices, and here we are. The draft opinion suggested there is a 5-4 Court majority to overturn Roe, and it's all too easy to imagine some 21st-century John Brown thinking he could prevent the ruling by assassinating a Justice. Now comes a dangerously close call.\par \par \uc2 The threat is especially acute this month as the Court winds down its term. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights protesters have been demonstrating in front of the Justices' homes. Public passions are running high, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nypost.com/2022/06/08/topless-pro-choice-protesters-crash-wnba-game-at-barclays-center-in-brooklyn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on Tuesday}}}\uc2  in New York topless pro-choice protestors rushed the court during a WNBA game. That may seem more stupid than threatening, but the protests are likely to get worse.\par \par \uc2 The Justices live in suburban Washington neighborhoods, not in gated compounds. They receive personal security as individuals, which has been enhanced since the leak. But their families don't, and Justice Amy Coney Barrett has school-age children.\par \par \uc2 If America is more polarized than at any point since the Civil War, as progressives often claim, then the political class needs to take the risk of violence more seriously. The Senate in early May unanimously passed a bill to enhance security for the Justices' families, in line with what's offered for high-ranking executive and legislative officers.\par \par \uc2 Yet the Supreme Court Police Parity Act has been languishing in the House. Democratic Sen. Dick Durbin (Ill.) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mkraju/status/1527286449925672961?ref_src=twsrc^tfw|twcamp^tweetembed|twterm^1527286449925672961|twgr^|twcon^s1_&ref_url=https://www.nationalreview.com/corner/top-senate-dem-very-much-concerned-pelosi-hasnt-passed-bill-to-protect-families-of-supreme-court-justices/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told a reporter}}}\uc2  recently that he's "very much" concerned by the delay. Senate GOP Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was right on Wednesday to call on Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and House Democrats to pass the bill immediately.\par \par \uc2 This is important for the Justices' personal safety and to show that the Court, as a vital institution of government, can't be compromised by threats or violence. No political party has a monopoly on political violence, and Mr. Roske is responsible for his actions.\par \par \uc2 But Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's 2020 remarks in front of the Supreme Court look even worse now than they did at the time. "I want to tell you, [Justice Neil] Gorsuch, I want to tell you, Kavanaugh. You have released the whirlwind, and you will pay the price," Mr. Schumer said. "You won't know what hit you if you go forward with these awful decisions."\par \par \uc2 Also notable is that the man outside the Kavanaugh home appears to have found the Justice's address online, and the locations of some of the Justices' homes were posted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist group Ruth Sent Us. The \uc2 Twitter\uc2  reaction from Ruth Sent Us included skepticism that Mr. Roske was even a threat: "Oh, what was this 'weapon' the 'California man' had? If it was a gun or even a knife, police would say so." They would, and they did.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Democrats now have a particular obligation to lower the public's blood pressure about the Court. They're predicting the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America, and that isn't close to what would happen if Roe is overturned. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate will merely move to the states and the political process in state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 Everyone on the right and left should be denouncing political violence, and that starts with President Biden. The consequences of a Supreme Court assassination are horrible even to contemplate.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/targeting-the-supreme-court-brett-kavanaugh-nicholas-john-roske-ruth-sent-us-roe-v-wade-11654725919"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Targeting the Supreme Court}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220608ei68008vh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
