\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Guns and the 'Throwaway Culture'; Democrats and Republicans reject policies that would fortify what St. John Paul II referred to as a 'culture of life.'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Timothy Dolan \par \uc2 711 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2022\par 22:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Like so many others, I am heartbroken by the senseless gun violence that has taken the lives of innocent victims in recent weeks. The loss is immeasurable. Americans of all political stripes are grieving and crying out to God to ask when our beloved nation will restore its respect for human life.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, our political system appears to be broken, as Republicans and Democrats operate in silos and see more political benefit in intransigence than consensus. The result is that both parties reject policies that would fortify what St. John Paul II referred to as a culture of life and instead embrace what Pope Francis has called a throwaway culture that extends even to human beings.\par \par \uc2 Among Republicans, we see it in the refusal to consider even widely popular proposals that would help to increase gun safety. Among Democrats, we see it in the frantic drive to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even in states where it would be impossible to expand it further.\par \par \uc2 Take my state, New York. Lawmakers in Albany recently scurried to pass a package of bills designed to increase the practice of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These proposals extended virtually no support to scared and overwhelmed pregnant women who might seek alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if only they knew they existed. Never mind that, though. With the Supreme Court poised to render a decision that could allow states to protect helpless human lives in the womb, New York lawmakers saw a political opportunity. They have stampeded to make the state an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  "sanctuary." They've passed measures to shield abortionists from liability and to encourage women from other states to come here to terminate the lives of their children. Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has unilaterally allocated millions of taxpayer dollars to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities to welcome out-of-state women.\par \par \uc2 What's next? Will they add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to the "I Love NY" tourism guide, alongside craft breweries and weekend getaway destinations? Would anyone be surprised if they did?\par \par \uc2 It gets worse. In addition to these new laws encouraging \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Legislature also passed a measure designed to eliminate pro-life options. That bill, now awaiting the governor's signature, would require the state health commissioner to "study" pro-life pregnancy centers, which exist to give women in crisis pregnancies a real alternative to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The goal is to intimidate these wonderful organizations into shutting down.\par \par \uc2 This is a complete rejection of the "choice" that elected representatives say they support. Where is the choice for the frightened young pregnant mom, usually African-American or Latina, who desperately wants to keep her baby but is having trouble paying her rent, putting food on the table, and filling her gas tank? Where is her sanctuary? Will the only answer for her be to destroy her offspring? And where is the sanctuary for the helpless baby in her womb, which should be the safest place around? Can't it be a "both and" rather than an "either or" when it comes to a mother and her baby? According to our state's elected Democrats, the answer appears to be no.\par \par \uc2 Republicans share the political blame. Toward the end of the legislative session, New York's elected Republicans opposed a raft of important new gun-safety laws, which passed anyway, promising to increase the legal age to purchase semiautomatic rifles and strengthen the state's red-flag law. My brother bishops and I were happy to support these important initiatives, all intended to save lives, just as we firmly opposed the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 How will it be possible to sustain a culture of nonviolence and safety when one party continues to applaud and promote the killing of defenseless babies in the womb and the other practically deifies firearms and rejects even the most common-sense regulations on them?\par \par \uc2 The prospects look bleak, but I'm in the business of hope. I ask New Yorkers of every faith to join me in prayer that our lawmakers will take seriously their responsibility to protect and defend human life.\par \par \uc2 Cardinal Dolan is Archbishop of New York.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-guns-and-the-throwaway-culture-cardinal-dolan-life-control-legislation-11654721312"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 , Guns and the 'Throwaway Culture'}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220608ei68008c1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
