\par\par \uc2 Television Review\par \uc2 Arts\par \b \uc2 'The Janes' Review: Women Underground; This HBO documentary about a clandestine network of illegal  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  providers in pre-Roe Chicago is gripping, regardless of where one stands on its central argument\b0\par\par\uc2 By John Anderson \par \uc2 955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2022\par 21:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 'We were criminals," says one of the subjects of "The Janes" on HBO. "We were felons." Her statement isn't a confession. It certainly isn't an act of contrition.\par \par \uc2 The Janes\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, 9 p.m., HBO\par \par \uc2 "The Janes" celebrates\u8212\'20\'14that's the only word\u8212\'20\'14the ad-hoc alliance of Chicago activists who facilitated or performed an estimated 11,000 abortions in the few years leading up to the Supreme Court's 1973 Roe v. Wade decision. The documentary by directors Tia Lessin and Emma Pildes\u8212\'20\'14a timely, enthralling if perhaps late-inning addition to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate\u8212\'20\'14may be intended as a hand-grenade lobbed into the culture wars. But it's a gripping historical document, regardless of where one stands on the central argument.\par \par \uc2 Much of the debate over the issue now seems to occur in the abstract, concerned with the sanctity of life and the metaphysics of conception. None of the members of the group that called itself Jane\u8212\'20\'14many of whom are identified even now by first name only\u8212\'20\'14address those points here. Nor, for that matter, do they bother to argue the realities that compelled them to do what they did: Sex was going to happen. Abortions were going to happen. Women were going to die. The group's objective was keeping women alive. There wasn't much to be debated as far as they were concerned.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lessin ("Trouble the Water") and Ms. Pildes, who's also a producer here, have a challenge on their hands: rendering what's largely an oral history into a compellingly visual production. Aside from the plentiful head-on interviews, the film is mostly a collage of period footage, a river of images that serves as a reflection, rather than evidence, of the stories being told. Which is fine, because the tales of the Janes, and the story of Jane, carries one along seamlessly enough into a sexual-political past that some people seem to have largely forgotten. If one is young enough, it may be a complete revelation. In addition, the narrative tension\u8212\'20\'14how long could they continue at what they were doing?\u8212\'20\'14makes "The Janes" a bit of a thriller.\par \par \uc2 The echoes of Prohibition America are inescapable: The interviewees argue that much of the "respect" for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws had to do with legality rather than morality, and Illinois legislation, inextricably linked to the Catholic Church, made abortions, as intended, hard to obtain. As "The Janes" vividly recalls, the Chicago mob had stepped into the void, providing unsafe procedures at extortionate rates and thus criminalizing desperate people who believed they had nowhere else to turn. Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors were motivated by a sense of mission; self-administered abortions often ended tragically. (One, involving carbolic acid, makes for a particularly harrowing anecdote.) Others were simply in it for the money.\par \par \uc2 Some weren't doctors at all, including "Mike," who became Jane's principal abortionist in the early days and had learned the trade, as it were, at the elbow of an actual surgeon. Mike, who is something of a character, says he had previously worked in construction. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a step up for me," he says.\par \par \uc2 "The Janes" isn't quite agitprop, but it may come as something of a shock to younger viewers, specifically younger women, that the kind of sexism recounted was so systemic as recently as the late '60s. (To contemporaries of the Janes, the response might be "no kidding.") Women could lose their jobs if they became pregnant\u8212\'20\'14\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was thus, for many, an economic-survival issue. As one Jane recalls, a classmate at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  was raped in her own bed at knifepoint and the college health services department provided nothing beyond a lecture on the victim's own "promiscuity." Part of the success of Jane, members recall, reflected the disbelief among male authorities that women would be/could be engaged in such an elaborate, years-long, clandestine operation.\par \par \uc2 As "Jody" tells the camera in one archival interview, the Janes felt a moral obligation to "disrespect a law that disrespected women," but as had been the case with the Volstead Act the authorities were inclined to turn a blind eye to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite or because of the fact that organized crime had been running the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  game in Chicago. "Who cares?" says homicide detective Ted O'Connor, recalling the sentiments of his fellow officers, who finally busted Jane in 1972. Two women had complained that their sister-in-law was getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the Chicago homicide squad was given the assignment. Mr. O'Connor's account is colored by his own evident embarrassment at having been involved at all, arresting a group of women for something that didn't seem to be his business. The Janes even had clergy among their allies\u8212\'20\'14the Clergy Consultation Service, a pandenominational group, came out in support of a woman's right to choose and made its own referrals for abortions. Jane's clients, members attest, included the wives, girlfriends and mistresses of policemen and politicians.\par \par \uc2 There's no mistaking the sentiments of "The Janes"\u8212\'20\'14its directors began making it in response to recent appointments to the Supreme Court and the realization that legal precedent was at risk. What they cover in their interviews with the activists, beyond good storytellers and astounding/appalling stories, is a great deal of territory that isn't being argued about now, but which is\u8212\'20\'14as both sides would uncharacteristically agree\u8212\'20\'14about life and death.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-janes-hbo-documentary-abortion-chicago-tia-lessin-emma-pildes-roe-vs-wade-1973-supreme-court-catholic-church-chicago-organized-crime-clergy-consultation-service-11654636745"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 'The Janes' Review: Women Underground }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220607ei67005xx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
