\par \par \uc2 Democratic Sen. \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  talks with employees, volunteers and board members of Lovering Health Center in Greenland, N.H. PHOTO: Kayana Szymczak for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 How voters weigh these issues could determine the fate of Ms. Hassan, who won her seat in 2016 by just over 1,000 votes. The race is one of Republicans' top targets as they hope to take control of the Senate, now split 50-50. Other possible pickups for Republicans include seats in Nevada, Arizona and Georgia, while Democrats are hoping to flip seats in Wisconsin and Pennsylvania.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://scholars.unh.edu/survey_center_polls/695/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A poll }}}\uc2  from the \uc2 University of New Hampshire\uc2  released Wednesday found 61% of the state's voters oppose overturning Roe v. Wade. One-fifth said they would be more likely to vote in the November elections if the decision is overturned. The survey also found 55% of voters said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hassan is highlighting her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and she is also keeping an eye on voters' concerns about the cost of gasoline and prescription drugs. Her first ad of the year is focused on her attempts to put in place a federal gas-tax holiday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/gas-tax-cut-hits-bipartisan-opposition-in-congress-11645016402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a proposal that has run into resistance }}}\uc2  from both Republicans and other Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The state has a Republican governor and GOP majorities in the state Legislature. At the same time, Democrats hold the state's two Senate seats and two House seats. After the state's September primary, both sides will be fighting to win over independent voters, who make up the state's largest voter share according to the secretary of state's office. 