\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 State Barriers to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Regardless of Roe v. Wade; A patchwork of U.S. state laws makes  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  less accessible for some women, no matter what the Supreme Court decides regarding the 1973 case\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kara Dapena \par \uc2 1,276 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2022\par 13:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A leaked draft opinion }}}\uc2  indicates that the Supreme Court might be prepared to overturn Roe v. Wade, the precedent that established federal constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nearly 50 years ago. But states have already instituted barriers, enacting 1,380 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions since Roe v. Wade was decided in 1973. Of those, 46% were enacted after 2010, though not all are in effect.\par \par \uc2 States enacted 89 new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in 2011 when the number of states where Republicans had control of the governorship, state senate and state house jumped from nine to 22 after the midterm elections. Nearly every state where new restrictions were enacted in 2011 were governed by Republican governors and state legislatures. Among Republican voters, 57% support overturning Roe, while 89% of Democrats oppose, according to the latest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/upholding-roe-v-wade-is-supported-by-most-americans-wsj-poll-finds-11654162200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WSJ-NORC poll }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If Roe were overturned, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 22 states }}}\uc2  have laws that could ban all or most abortions, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics. Those laws include pre-Roe bans that were never removed, so-called trigger bans that could go into effect after a Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe and other laws that would ban most abortions.\par \par \uc2 With or without a change to Roe, other barriers exist. About 38% of reproductive-age women lived in a county without an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic as of 2017, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://data.guttmacher.org/states/table?state=US+AL+AK+AZ+AR+CA+CO+CT+DE+DC+FL+GA+HI+ID+IL+IN+IA+KS+KY+LA+ME+MD+MA+MI+MN+MS+MO+MT+NE+NV+NH+NJ+NM+NY+NC+ND+OH+OK+OR+PA+RI+SC+SD+TN+TX+UT+VT+VA+WA+WV+WI+WY&topics=59&dataset=data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute }}}\uc2 . Wyoming, Mississippi and West Virginia topped the list, with at least 90% of women of reproductive age in those states living in counties without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Gestational limits\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision in Roe v. Wade instituted the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability, which is the point at which a fetus can live outside the womb. All but six states have laws in effect that prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a specified point in pregnancy. An average pregnancy lasts approximately 40 weeks from the start of a woman's last menstrual period to birth.\par \par \uc2 All 44 states with gestational limits have exceptions if the patient's life is at risk, and most have additional exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Nine out of 10 abortions are performed at 13 weeks or earlier, according to the federal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/70/ss/ss7009a1.htm#T10_down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention }}}\uc2 , and less than 1% are performed after 20 weeks. In the six states without gestational limits, 89% are performed at 13 weeks or earlier and 3% are performed after 20 weeks, according to the CDC. Before six-week limits went into effect in Texas in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-takes-effect-pending-supreme-court-action-11630498466?mod=series_sntexasabortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 2021 }}}\uc2  and Oklahoma in May, about 60% were performed after that point in those states in 2019, according to the CDC. On May 25, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/oklahoma-governor-signs-abortion-ban-11653529937"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oklahoma's governor }}}\uc2  signed into law a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that took effect immediately.\par \par \uc2 Counseling and ultrasounds\par \par \uc2 Twenty-seven states require counseling followed by a waiting period before a woman may undergo the procedure. The waiting-period length is typically 24 hours, but ranges from 18 to 72 hours depending on the state. In all states it can be waived in a medical emergency or when the patient's life or health is threatened.\par \par \uc2 An ultrasound is mandatory before the procedure in 17 states. Ultrasounds aren't medically necessary for most patients and shouldn't be universally mandated, according to the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 . Ultrasounds can aid in determining gestational age and screening for fetal anomalies. They can also evaluate suspected ectopic pregnancies, but they aren't necessary for patients who don't have signs, symptoms or risk factors, according to the \uc2 ACOG\uc2 , and can create an additional financial burden on a patient.\par \par \uc2 Among states where a fetal ultrasound is mandatory, some require the provider to display and describe the ultrasound image, while others require the provider to offer the patient the option to view it. A few also mandate that an ultrasound be performed at least 24 hours before the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure.\par \par \uc2 Hospital setting\par \par \uc2 In some states, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be performed in a hospital if it is sought after a specified point in pregnancy. That point in gestation tends to be fetal viability, but ranges from 20 weeks in North Carolina to the third trimester in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Coverage\par \par \uc2 An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might cost up to $750 in the first trimester and double that amount later in pregnancy, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthood.org/learn/ask-experts/how-much-does-it-cost-to-get-an-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Planned Parenthood }}}\uc2 . About half of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients in 2014 lived below the federal income poverty rate. The procedure could be free or low-cost with health insurance, but some states restrict some insurance coverage. Just over half of patients paid for the procedure out-of-pocket in 2014 despite nearly three-quarters having health insurance, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/report/characteristics-us-abortion-patients-2014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Medicaid, a public health-insurance program for low-income and disabled people, covers \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some states under some circumstances. More than a third of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients in 2014 had Medicaid coverage, but only a quarter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients used Medicaid as a payment method, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/report/characteristics-us-abortion-patients-2014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute }}}\uc2 . Thirty-four states and the District of Columbia restrict Medicaid coverage for abortions.\par \par \uc2 Those 34 states and the District of Columbia allow exceptions for life endangerment, rape and incest except for South Dakota, which limits the exception to life endangerment.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a third of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients had private insurance in 2014, but only 15% of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients used private insurance as a payment method, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/report/characteristics-us-abortion-patients-2014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute }}}\uc2 . Three out of five privately insured \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients paid out of pocket for the procedure, which could be for a variety of reasons, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/gpr/2018/06/restrictions-private-insurance-coverage-abortion-danger-abortion-access-and-better-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute }}}\uc2 . Their plan could have a high deductible, their provider could be out-of-network, they might worry about their insurer, employer or family finding out about the procedure, the patient could be unaware their plan covers \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or they might have a plan that doesn't cover the procedure.\par \par \uc2 States regulate fully insured private health plans, and 11 states restrict these plans' coverage of abortions.\par \par \uc2 All these 11 states have exceptions for life endangerment. And there are some additional exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Refusal\par \par \uc2 Forty-four states allow various healthcare institutions to refuse to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 In some states, only religious or private healthcare institutions can refuse to provide these services. Most states allow any healthcare institution to refuse.\par \par \uc2 All of those 44 states, plus Connecticut and Rhode Island, also allow individual healthcare providers to refuse to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 More than two-thirds of Americans want to uphold Roe v. Wade, and most favor women having access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason, according to the latest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/upholding-roe-v-wade-is-supported-by-most-americans-wsj-poll-finds-11654162200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WSJ-NORC poll }}}\uc2 . But about half of Americans aren't sure how a reversal of Roe would affect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prri.org/spotlight/most-oppose-overturning-roe-v-wade-widespread-confusion-over-a-post-roe-world/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March survey }}}\uc2  of 2,726 adults by the Public Religion Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Methodology\par \par \uc2 State-level policies and annual restrictions enacted are according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . Policies are as of May 1, aside from Oklahoma's ban throughout pregnancy, and not all exceptions have been noted. Reproductive-age women are those 15 to 44 years old, according to the Census Bureau's American Community Survey five-year estimates for 2020, and calculated as a share of the total number of women in the age group in the 50 U.S. states plus Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What is the future for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the U.S.? Join the conversation below.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-barrier-abortion-roe-v-wade-11654170644"}}{