\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Upholding Roe v. Wade Is Supported by Most Americans, WSJ Poll Finds; After decades of shifting attitudes, some 57% of respondents say a woman should be able to get an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  for any reason\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey \par \uc2 1,279 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2022\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14More than two-thirds of Americans want to uphold Roe v. Wade, and most favor women having {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-state-abortion-laws-stand-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11651614828"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access to legal \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  for any reason, according to a new Wall Street Journal poll that shows a four-decade evolution in the country's viewpoints regarding the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The findings are from a Journal poll conducted with NORC at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 , a nonpartisan research organization that measures social attitudes. The poll was taken after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-wade-abortion-supreme-court-explainer-11651601806"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leak of a draft opinion }}}\uc2  that suggests the Supreme Court might be preparing to overturn the 1973 decision that established the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the survey, 68% of respondents said they wouldn't like to see the court completely overturn Roe, while 30% said they support that move.\par \par \uc2 The survey shows Americans have more-mixed feelings about restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access based on the length of the pregnancy. On banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, 34% of respondents were in support, 43% were in opposition and 21% said they neither supported nor opposed such a move. The numbers were similar for a ban after six weeks, with 30% of respondents in favor, 49% opposed and 19% with no view. A Journal poll in March {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/support-for-15-week-abortion-ban-outweighs-opposition-wsj-poll-finds-11648821601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found plurality support }}}\uc2  for a 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 "There's still obviously a lot of nuance in people's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opinions," said Jennifer Benz, vice president of public affairs and media research at NORC. She added that research shows that many people who say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal for any reason also have limits on that view, especially when it comes to abortions later in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some 57% of respondents said a woman should be able to obtain a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if she wants it for any reason, the highest share since NORC began asking the question every few years starting in 1977. The share opposed to a woman having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  simply because she wants one, 41%, was the lowest on record. The poll's 4 percentage point margin of error means that, while those results have changed significantly over time, they might not be statistically different from last year's findings.\par \par \uc2 This increased support for, and declining opposition to, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for any reason occurred slowly from the late 1970s until the late 2010s, when the change in attitudes began accelerating.\par \par \uc2 The opinions of many Americans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are coming under a spotlight as the Supreme Court considers whether to allow a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-supreme-court-weighs-abortion-rights-more-states-look-at-15-week-bans-11645641664"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 15-week ban passed in Mississippi }}}\uc2 , which is at odds with Roe and other precedents. A report last year by the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  estimated that about 93% of abortions in the U.S. in 2019 took place by 15 weeks of gestation.\par \par \uc2 The high court has previously established the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until a fetus is able to sustain meaningful life outside of the womb, which generally occurs at weeks 22 to 24 of pregnancy. Some states plan to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access if Roe is overturned. Others have passed laws seeking to limit the procedure that are currently blocked by state and federal courts and could go into effect if Roe were overturned. Oklahoma and Texas have in the past year implemented bans on performing abortions after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 "I personally don't agree with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but I don't think there should be laws banning women from having abortions. The option should be available," said Katrina Jones, 46 years old, of Fort Worth, Texas, who said she didn't identify with a political party. "In my state, it's being used as campaign talk. I don't think it's sincere about saving children's lives."\par \par \uc2 Norine Woodruff, 63, of Forest Lake, Minn., who said she typically votes Republican, said she previously supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but changed her views over the years. She said she supports overturning Roe and letting states put laws in place, adding that she would support some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in instances of rape or incest, serious birth defects or risks to the mother's health.\par \par \uc2 "My opinion has changed dramatically from the late '70s and early '80s, when I had at least five or six friends that had them. Each one of them, I ended up crying with them," she said. "It just went too far."\par \par \uc2 Americans seem to have little confidence in their state leaders, who might determine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access around the country going forward. Asked who should be responsible for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, 44% said the Supreme Court; 20%, state legislatures and governors; 17%, Congress; and 15%, state courts.\par \par \uc2 They also held some concerns about the high court, with 66% saying Supreme Court decisions were based more on the members' political views, compared with 33% who said their rulings were based on the Constitution and the law. Positive views of the court have declined in recent years, according to past Journal polls conducted with NBC News.\par \par \uc2 "They're most definitely following political leanings," Zachary Lindahl, 35, of Henderson, N.C., said of the high court. He said he considers himself an independent but has been voting Democratic. He supports keeping Roe in place, he said, and isn't comfortable with elected officials or courts dictating access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "What happens if we get another conservative Congress?" he asked.\par \par \uc2 The WSJ-NORC poll showed broader support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when women face health risks and financial challenges. A total of 86% of respondents supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access if the mother's health is at serious risk; 84% if the pregnancy is the result of rape or incest; and 76% if there is a strong chance of a serious birth defect. Fifty-nine percent said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be possible if the family has a low income and can't afford more children.\par \par \uc2 "I think there should be some criteria on it. I don't think it should be any old reason," said Freddie Roddy, 59, of El Cajon, Calif., a Democrat who said he thought Roe should remain in place. "I think at one point, when the fetus gets a certain age, you're taking a life at that point."\par \par \uc2 In the survey, Republicans were more likely to favor state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions at six or 15 weeks than Democrats, and rural Americans were more likely to support a ban at six or 15 weeks than those who live in urban areas. Those older than 45 were twice as likely to back a 15-week ban as those between ages 18 and 29.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do you expect the Supreme Court to narrow or overturn Roe v. Wade? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Amy Hill, 31, of Shady Spring, W.Va., who said she has voted Republican, said that she was only comfortable permitting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of rape. "I think sex is voluntary if you aren't raped," she said. "If you are voluntarily having sex with people, you should take the precaution to wear a condom or be on birth control."\par \par \uc2 Men and women were equally likely to support upholding Roe. There was more backing for overturning the decision among older age groups. Among those age 65 and older, 35% would like a complete overturn of Roe, compared with 24% among those ages 18 to 29.\par \par \uc2 The WSJ-NORC poll surveyed 1,071 adults from May 9 through May 17.\par \par \uc2 Write to Catherine Lucey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:catherine.lucey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catherine.lucey@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/upholding-roe-v-wade-is-supported-by-most-americans-wsj-poll-finds-11654162200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Upholding Roe v. Wade Is Supported by Most Americans, WSJ Poll Finds  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220602ei62000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
