\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Michigan Judge Suspends Enforcement of Pre-Roe  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban; Court finds that dormant 91-year-old state law violates state's constitution\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Jacob Gershman \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par 21:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A Michigan judge on Tuesday ruled that the state may not enforce a dormant 91-year-old state law banning most abortions should the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  no longer recognize a woman's right to terminate her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The ruling by the chief judge on Michigan's Court of Claims, Elizabeth Gleicher, held that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a fundamental right under the state's own constitution.\par \par \uc2 "After 50 years of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Michigan, there can be no doubt but that the right of personal autonomy and bodily integrity enjoyed by our citizens includes the right of a woman, in consultation with her physician, to terminate a pregnancy," the judge wrote in a 27-page ruling, granting a preliminary injunction sought by Planned Parenthood of Michigan and a local \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Michigan is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-state-abortion-laws-stand-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11651614828?page=3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of eight states  }}}\uc2 , including Arizona, West Virginia and Wisconsin, that have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that predate Roe v. Wade, the 1973 Supreme Court decision that first made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal nationwide, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit comes as states are bracing for a decision from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, expected by the end of June. The high court is considering whether to allow a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mississippi-asks-supreme-court-to-end-roe-v-wade-abortion-rights-11626985810?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mississippi law banning \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, roughly two months earlier than current Supreme Court precedent allows. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-wade-abortion-supreme-court-explainer-11651601806?page=3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked draft opinion  }}}\uc2  earlier this month indicated that the Supreme Court could overturn Roe, potentially allowing states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans such as Michigan's have been unenforceable for the past five decades. If the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturns Roe, there are concerns that currently dormant bans could become broadly enforceable again.\par \par \uc2 The 1931 Michigan law makes providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any point during pregnancy a felony that can potentially result in up to four years in prison. In 1973, the Michigan Supreme Court interpreted the statute to be unenforceable to the extent it conflicts with Roe.\par \par \uc2 In her ruling, Judge Gleicher stated that due-process protections in Michigan's Constitution imply a right to bodily integrity that would be violated by the 1931 law.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  has long vowed to do everything in its power to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and this ruling in Michigan is a victory in our effort to make sure nothing comes between patients and their freedom to control their own bodies, lives, and futures," said \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2  , president and chief executive of \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Judge Gleicher's court has limited jurisdiction to hear civil actions, including constitutional claims, brought against the state.\par \par \uc2 In April, Judge Gleicher disclosed to lawyers involved in the litigation that she has made annual donations to Planned Parenthood of Michigan and once represented \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  as a volunteer attorney in the 1990s. The judge said she was confident of her objectivity in the matter and didn't think a recusal was warranted.\par \par \uc2 While the preliminary injunction precludes enforcement of the law for now, its longer-term future remains unclear. The \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  lawsuit was against the state's Democratic attorney general, Dana Nessel, who declined to defend the law. On Tuesday, she called the preliminary injunction "a victory for the millions of Michigan women fighting for their right to bodily integrity and personal freedom."\par \par \uc2 Two Michigan antiabortion groups, Right to Life of Michigan and the Michigan Catholic Conference, filed a brief with the court arguing the case should be dismissed because no one was defending the law.\par \par \uc2 The two groups are considering their next steps, said \uc2 John Bursch\uc2  , vice president of appellate advocacy at \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  , which represented the organizations.\par \par \uc2 "The court's ruling is egregious for many reasons," Mr. Bursch said. "Michigan citizens are entitled to neutral decision makers, and government officials have a duty to uphold the law and protect their citizens, including unborn children."\par \par \uc2 Michigan Democratic Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  has filed separate legal challenges to the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, seeking to get the Michigan Supreme Court to declare that the law violates the state constitution. The governor said Tuesday that she will continue to pursue those efforts. "While today's preliminary injunction offers immediate, critical relief, we need the Michigan Supreme Court to weigh in and establish the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under our state constitution," she said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jacob Gershman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jacob.gershman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jacob.gershman@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jacob Gershman at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigan-judge-suspends-enforcement-of-pre-roe-abortion-ban-11652819032"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michigan Judge Suspends Enforcement of Pre-Roe \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ban  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220517ei5h00691\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
