\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 -Rights Supporters March to Protest Possible Overturning of Roe v. Wade; Thousands turn out at events in New York, Washington, D.C., Chicago, Los Angeles and Austin, Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amara Omeokwe and Ian Talley in Washington, Ginger Adams Otis and Will Parker in New York, and Elizabeth Findell in Austin \par \uc2 1,265 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par 20:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Crowds of pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protesters chanted, carried banners and marched in cities across the country Saturday, in the first large public response to a draft Supreme Court opinion indicating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the high court may overturn Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2 , the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 More than 380 rallies organized under the slogan "Bans Off Our Bodies" were planned Saturday, including in New York, Los Angeles, Washington, D.C., Chicago, and Austin. The aim is to pressure state and federal lawmakers to safeguard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights regardless of what the Supreme Court decides, organizers said.\par \par \uc2 At the National Mall in Washington, what appeared to be several thousand protesters supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\u8212\'20\'14young and old but predominantly women\u8212\'20\'14gathered under overcast skies as they prepared to march up Constitution Avenue to the Supreme Court. Among the crowd's chants were "I believe we will win" and "Keep your hands off our bodies."\par \par \uc2 Some demonstrators there said they didn't expect the protest to affect the court's actions, yet they still felt compelled to voice their dissent.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's very important that we have the right to our own body and that when I grow up and go out in the world, I'm able to make decisions for myself and put my own health over anything else," said Beth Sopp, an 18-year-old Virginia high-school student at the Washington rally.\par \par \uc2 Though the marches weren't explicitly partisan, some attendees said they were focused on electing more Democrats, most of whom support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 "Hopefully, at election time in November, we'll get some more Democratic seats," said Cheryl Farver, a 65-year-old retired social worker from Owosso, Mich.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How might \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights marches affect the national conversation around the pending Supreme Court decision? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Later in the afternoon, marchers reached the Supreme Court, where an 8-foot-tall steel mesh barrier surrounded the courthouse. A small group of counterprotesters shouting "Hey hey, ho ho, Roe v. Wade has got to go" were quickly surrounded by a throng of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters, who drowned out the chants.\par \par \uc2 Saturday's events were organized by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights and labor groups, including Planned Parenthood, UltraViolet, Women's March, MoveOn and the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , or \uc2 SEIU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democratic Rep. Barbara Lee of California spoke to the crowd in Washington. Event organizers there said they had obtained a permit for up to 17,000 people, though more than that number had registered to attend.\par \par \uc2 In New York City, sign-waving protesters led by a pounding drumline packed onto the Brooklyn Bridge and marched slowly into lower Manhattan. When the crowd arrived at Foley Square in front of several courthouses, the mood turned from protest to party. Demonstrators danced to a live band and food trucks handed out free meals.\par \par \uc2 Ilona Kramer, a 35-year-old from Brooklyn, was there with her two young children and was waiting for her 94-year-old grandmother to join them. "It's really hard to see something that my grandmother and my mother fought for in the course of their lifetime so imperiled," Ms. Kramer said.\par \par \uc2 About 1,000 people appeared to gather in Foley Square before afternoon rains drove many away.\par \par \uc2 Prominent New York Democrats including Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and Rep. \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2  also attended the rally.\par \par \uc2 In Austin, Texas, a crowd that appeared to number in the thousands filled the Capitol's front lawn in the midday heat. Signs they carried read "WTF SCOTUS" and "You wouldn't force children to wear masks, how can you force one to have a child?" One simply said: "I am very upset!!"\par \par \uc2 Teresa Bonilla, an insurance agent and Army wife who lives at Fort Hood in Killeen, Texas, came with her three daughters.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bonilla teared up as she described having two abortions herself\u8212\'20\'14the first against her will when she was 16 and the second when her eldest daughter was young and she and her husband weren't in a position to handle another. "That's why it's important for my children to have choices," she said.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates spoke from a stage in front of the Capitol's pink dome. Wendy Davis, a former Democratic state senator {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424127887324328204578568750147308138"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who became famous with a filibuster }}}\uc2  to stop an antiabortion bill in 2013, shared her thoughts about seeing such efforts potentially reversed.\par \par \uc2 "Behind us," she said, referring to the Capitol building where Texas legislators work, "there was a group of people working to do everything they could to make sure that the promise we'd told our daughters their whole life was a lie\u8230\'20\'26I cannot even believe we are here."\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is expected to issue its ruling in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-mississippi-abortion-case-at-the-supreme-court-what-you-should-know-11621337351"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization }}}\uc2  by the end of June. A leaked draft of its decision indicated the court would overturn Roe v. Wade. The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-confirms-authenticity-of-leaked-draft-opinion-overruling-roe-v-wade-11651592725"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 confirmed the draft's authenticity }}}\uc2  and said it wasn't final.\par \par \uc2 If the court overturns Roe, about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/states-move-to-restrict-or-protect-abortion-rights-after-leak-of-draft-opinion-overturning-roe-v-wade-11651608822"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 22 states are poised to ban or severely restrict the procedure }}}\uc2 , according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a policy group that tracks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law and supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. More than a dozen states including California and New York, meanwhile, have passed state laws strengthening access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 "We want people in the streets to show our collective anger at this moment, but also plug into ways they can help \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, help defend providers, get registered to vote or learn about what policies they can advocate for," said Sonja Spoo, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigns at UltraViolet, one of several groups planning the events.\par \par \uc2 Only a few counterprotesters were present at the start of the march in Washington, D.C. Police were present but largely stayed back from the proceedings.\par \par \uc2 Students for Life, a group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, planned to counterprotest at several of the events in cities including Washington, Phoenix, Austin and Atlanta, said Lori Cascio, a field operations manager for the group. Ms. Cascio said about 50 members of the organization intended to be outside the Supreme Court around noon Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "You have to be visible if you want your side of the story to be told," Ms. Cascio said. "That's why we show up."\par \par \uc2 At the Washington event, Rose Brunache, president of the local chapter of the \uc2 National Organization for Women\uc2 , said she hoped more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters would now feel motivated to vote in the midterms. Ms. Brunache said she and other activists would be building resources to assist women in states that are likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the Supreme Court issues the expected ruling.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to have to raise a lot of money because a lot of women from out of state are going to come to this area, to the Northeast in general," she said.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Calfas contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Amara Omeokwe at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:amara.omeokwe@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 amara.omeokwe@wsj.com }}}\uc2 , Ian Talley at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:ian.talley@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ian.talley@wsj.com }}}\uc2 , Ginger Adams Otis at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Ginger.AdamsOtis@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ginger.AdamsOtis@wsj.com }}}\uc2 , Will Parker at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:will.parker@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will.parker@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Elizabeth Findell at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-rights-marches-planned-across-u-s-to-protest-possible-overturning-of-roe-v-wade-11652520603"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 -Rights Supporters March to Protest Possible Overturning of Roe v. Wade }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220514ei5e000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
