\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  and the Economy; The Treasury Secretary ignores the value of lost human capital.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par 22:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  visited the Senate on Tuesday to answer questions about risks to financial stability such as inflation, rising interest rates and volatile asset prices. But Democrats were more interested in her views on the risk that the Supreme Court might overturn Roe v. Wade. The exchange wasn't helpful to their cause.\par \par \uc2 "What impact will the loss of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access mean economically for women?" New Jersey Sen. \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  asked Ms. Yellen. She replied with a reductionist economic argument: "I believe eliminating the right of women to make decisions about when and whether to have children would have very damaging effects on the economy and would set women back by decades."\par \par \uc2 She added: "Roe v. Wade and access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped lead to increased labor force participation" of women and "research also shows that it had a favorable impact on the well-being and earnings of children."\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republican Tim Scott rejoined: "Did you say that ending the life of a child is good for the labor force participation rate?" Ms. Yellen demurred but warned that restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would limit women's ability to "plan lives that are fulfilling and satisfying" and lead to more unwanted children especially by black women, "who will grow up in poverty and do worse themselves."\par \par \uc2 Putting the difficult moral choice of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such utilitarian terms won't win over many converts. But even on those narrow terms, Ms. Yellen ignores some economic facts.\par \par \uc2 The link between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and women's labor force participation is more tenuous than she suggests. Women's labor participation was rising for decades before Roe and continued to climb until the early 2000s amid cultural changes. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates have also fallen by half since 1980, and teen pregnancy rates have plunged by two-thirds since 1990 amid increased access to contraception. Far more than access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  influences the life and work choices of women.\par \par \uc2 More fundamental, Ms. Yellen overlooks the lost productive contribution of children who were never born. People are assets, the source of inventions and new businesses. Human capital is crucial to economic growth and a dynamic society. China has abandoned its one-child policy, which sometimes included coerced abortions, because it foresees a declining population in the years ahead.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a fraught moral issue, which is why it ought to be settled democratically, rather than by judicial fiat. But its consequences can't be measured by lifetime earnings or the labor participation rate.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/janet-yellen-on-abortion-and-the-economy-tim-scott-senate-roe-v-wade-labor-force-participation-11652300307"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Janet Yellen on \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  and the Economy}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220511ei5b00899\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
