\par\par \uc2 Politics & Ideas\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Democrats' Roe v. Wade Pitfall; Activists push both parties toward the extremes on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14and away from most voters.\b0\par\par\uc2 By William A. Galston \par \uc2 858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par 16:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The distinct possibility that the Supreme Court will overturn Roe v. Wade outright has triggered\u8212\'20\'14predictably\u8212\'20\'14a polarized debate that is out of step with the views of most Americans. Both political parties can go too far and\u8212\'20\'14if past is prologue\u8212\'20\'14probably will. Democrats hope to make gains on this issue but could forfeit them if they yield to pressure from activist groups.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with the top-line figures. Public opinion on Roe hasn't budged in the past quarter-century: A solid but not overwhelming majority support the decision, while a staunch minority oppose it. Not surprisingly, recent polls show that a majority of Americans oppose overturning the decision.\par \par \uc2 But this binary choice conceals more than it reveals about public attitudes on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Few Americans believe that this practice should be legal in all circumstances, and even fewer think that it should be prohibited outright. According to a recent Economist/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  survey, only 5% of Americans\u8212\'20\'14including 9% of Republicans and 10% of conservatives\u8212\'20\'14believe that a woman should never be able to obtain a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For most Americans, the circumstances are decisive.\par \par \uc2 Timing is crucial. A recent Yahoo/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll found that while 61% of Americans believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be "generally legal" during the first three months of pregnancy, this figure falls to 32% for the second trimester and 19% for the third, a stance somewhat to the right of what Roe allows. A \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  survey found that, absent special circumstances, Americans oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a 2-to-1 margin after 24 weeks, when the fetus has reached the age of viability.\par \par \uc2 If timing makes a difference in the public's response to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so do the reasons for it. Several surveys conducted after the draft Supreme Court decision was leaked reach essentially the same conclusion. A supermajority of Americans would permit abortions in cases of rape, incest, and threats to the life of the mother, and when there is evidence of serious birth defects. But majorities reject abortions for reasons such as economic privation, not wanting more children, or not wanting to marry the father.\par \par \uc2 For most Americans, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises deep issues about morality\u8212\'20\'14and about the relationship between morality and law. Pew found that while 47% see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as morally wrong in most or all cases, only 22% say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal whenever it is immoral. Nearly half of all adults\u8212\'20\'14including many who believe that human life begins at conception\u8212\'20\'14think that there are circumstances in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is morally wrong but should nonetheless be permitted by the law.\par \par \uc2 Many Democrats believe that if the Supreme Court overrules Roe by the end of its current term, the backlash could shift the dynamic of the midterm election in their favor, and there is evidence to support this hypothesis. As recently as last November, according to the Yahoo/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll, only 4% of Democrats regarded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their most important issue. Now, this figure has soared to 20% (topping even healthcare and climate change) and includes 22% of Democratic women as well as 27% of liberal Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Although Republicans typically care more about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Democrats, recent developments have turned this on its head. Only 6% of Republican voters cite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their most important issue, and 28% of pro-choice voters say that they will support only candidates who share their views on this issue, compared with 21% for pro-life voters. Contrary to conventional wisdom, Democrats and liberals are more unified around a pro-choice stance than Republicans and conservatives are around the pro-life alternative.\par \par \uc2 Developments at the state level could raise the salience of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even higher. Many states have so-called trigger laws, bans that would take effect soon after the court's decision. Others\u8212\'20\'14including the swing states of Wisconsin, Michigan, and Arizona\u8212\'20\'14have pre-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that could be enforced as soon as Roe falls.\par \par \uc2 While many Republican elected officials favor overturning Roe because doing so would "return the issue to the states," antiabortion activists have signaled their opposition to a patchwork of diverse state statutes. They are pushing instead for an outright national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which 69% of Americans oppose. Public discussion of this prospect will further arouse Democrats and could force Republican candidates to choose between their base and swing voters in contested states and districts.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, 42% of Democrats and 54% of liberals agree with the proposition that "\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should always be legal" and that "there should be no restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a stance that three-quarters of Americans reject. When activists morphed the reasonable demand for criminal-justice reform into "Defund the police," Democrats lost control of the issue. It could happen again.\par \par \uc2 For Democrats, shifting the focus of the midterm elections away from inflation, crime, and immigration toward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Republican extremism should be a no-brainer\u8212\'20\'14if they can avoid becoming the party of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-democrats-abortion-pitfall-demand-activists-trimester-first-third-polling-voters-11652190689"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Democrats' Roe v. Wade Pitfall}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220510ei5a004ed\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
