\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Schumer's Radical  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bill; The Women's Health Protection Act makes the new Democratic policy: Safe, legal and don't tell your parents.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 796 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par 22:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Protesters marched on the homes of conservative Supreme Court Justices over the weekend, an ugly attempt to scare them into saving Roe v. Wade. This week the drama moves to the Senate, where Democratic Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  plans a vote on a sweeping bill to override state laws and set a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats passed the Women's Health Protection Act (WHPA) last year, but it stalled in the Senate. It's expected to fail again this week. But that isn't stopping Mr. Schumer, who is refusing to take up a bill by GOP Sens. \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski, who have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.collins.senate.gov/newsroom/senators-collins-and-murkowski-introduce-bill-to-codify-supreme-court-decisions-on-reproductive-rights_roe-v-wade-and-planned-parenthood-v-casey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their own proposal}}}\uc2  to codify Roe v. Wade. "I have long supported a woman's right to choose," Ms. Murkowski said, "but my position is not without limits, and this partisan Women's Health Protection Act simply goes too far."\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Bill Clinton's artful framing was that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be "safe, legal and rare," but that's ancient history to today's Democrats. The WHPA would guarantee \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access "at any point or points in time prior to fetal viability," about 23 weeks. Women seeking such services could not be asked to "disclose the patient's reason." Some states have tried to prohibit sex-selective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the practice usually of terminating a girl merely because a boy is desired. The WHPA appears to protect that choice.\par \par \uc2 After fetal viability, the WHPA would assure a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever the physician's "good-faith medical judgment" is that "the pregnancy would pose a risk to the pregnant patient's life or health." What counts as "health"? This is sometimes defined to include mental, emotional or familial factors, a loophole that permits elective abortions, more or less, through all nine months of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The legislation also exempts itself from the Religious Freedom Restoration Act, which is why Ms. Collins says it would undercut "basic conscience protections" for religious healthcare providers. In its findings, the bill says \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access "has been obstructed" by state "parental involvement laws (notification and consent)."\par \par \uc2 Is the Democratic policy in 2022 that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be safe, legal and don't tell your parents? "Ultimately I feel that young women at a certain age should have the rights to make these kind of decisions with their doctor," Arizona Sen. Mark Kelly told \uc2 National Review\uc2  reporter John McCormack. "I'm not going to be the arbiter of an age and a timeline." Nobody is asking him to be the arbiter. Yet he's voting to nullify state laws.\par \par \uc2 A national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill is also constitutionally suspect. If the Supreme Court overturns Roe, the federal government will lack any 14th Amendment justification to override state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. The WHPA could be left relying on Congress's power to "regulate Commerce" among the states.\par \par \uc2 But the Commerce Clause isn't unlimited, and Congress can't overrule the constitutional police powers of the 50 states. It can ban some activity that a state allows, such as marijuana cultivation in California (Gonzales v. Raich, 2005) when there is arguably an interstate market in the drug. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a medical procedure provided and regulated locally or by states.\par \par \uc2 Some states are likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe falls. If Congress can then compel the legality of abortions that are banned by state law, there is no limiting principle to what traditional sphere of state power it can't oversee under the Commerce Clause. Why not local zoning or prostitution laws?\par \par \uc2 By the way, in voting for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, Democrats may be creating an open door for Republicans to do the same when they next hold power. This would be as constitutionally dubious as Mr. Schumer's bill, but Democrats will have made it easier for the GOP to ignore the Constitution too.\par \par \uc2 Similar logic probably applies to the Partial-Birth \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ban Act that Congress passed in 2003. The Justices upheld that law against a different set of arguments. Yet as Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  noted in a concurrence: "Whether the Act constitutes a permissible exercise of Congress' power under the Commerce Clause is not before the Court." If Roe falls, it's hard to justify under the Commerce Clause.\par \par \uc2 As for Democratic calls to kill the Senate filibuster, do they really want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in 50 states to flip-flop depending on who wins the next Senate race in Georgia or Wisconsin? The wise move is to table the WHPA. Then Democrats can fight it out in the states, the constitutional way, for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy they want.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/chuck-schumers-radical-abortion-bill-senate-democrats-roe-v-wade-womens-health-protection-act-11652133702"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Schumer's Radical \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Bill}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220509ei59005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
