\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Europe's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Lesson; How democracies compromised on the issue after political debate, not judicial fiat.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 622 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par 21:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 American progressives, and some on the right, have convinced themselves that legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will disappear the moment the Supreme Court reverses its Roe v. Wade precedent. Since the Court is contemplating this, readers might appreciate examples from democracies that have grappled with this difficult issue without nine Justices to tell them what to do.\par \par \uc2 We mean Europe, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in most countries, usually with limits that are more strict than America's and generally as a result of democratic choice.\par \par \uc2 The notable feature of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Europe is that each country has tailored its laws to local mores after political debate. Britain and the Netherlands are among the more permissive, allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under most circumstances up to 24 weeks of pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14and at taxpayer expense in \uc2 Britain's National Health Service\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Poland and a handful of small, majority-Catholic countries are at the other end of the spectrum, banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under most circumstances although women can avail of free movement within the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to travel to another country.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Sweden is available on-demand up to the 18th week, and after that only with medical permission if the fetus isn't viable. In Italy it's in the first 90 days. Many countries, such as Denmark, Germany and Belgium, allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand up to the 12th week, while France recently extended it to 14 and in Portugal it's 10. Countries tend to apply stricter limits after those times, such as requiring sign-off from multiple doctors or allowing later abortions only if the mother's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 Without court rulings mandating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, European voters by and large have chosen to permit it in a way that would disappoint American pro-lifers. But even liberal and largely secular Europeans impose the sort of limitation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that America's pro-choice left claims to find intolerable. Mississippi's ban, which is the law at issue now at the Supreme Court, begins after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 European laws also include waiting periods for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some countries, such as seven days in Italy and three in Germany. Denmark and the Netherlands are among several countries that require parental consent before minors can obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Germany and Belgium require counseling first.\par \par \uc2 Keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics in the democratic sphere rather than the courts has prevented it from becoming a destructive front in the culture war. This doesn't mean \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are immutable. Ireland in 2018 legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  via a referendum, while Germany recently lifted a ban on "advertising" \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. But such changes happen as social mores change and via persuasion of undecided lawmakers and voters rather than shrill fundraising letters. They tend to represent a consensus that has a chance to remain stable for some time.\par \par \uc2 Note that European \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has mostly ended up where opinion polls suggest most Americans would prefer to be: with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal in the first trimester but with more restrictions later, and with some checks such as a waiting period or parental notification for minors. The main \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lesson from Europe is that voters can be trusted with such an important issue.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  rules on Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization as last week's leak suggests, this new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics will be an adjustment for partisans\u8212\'20\'14on both sides\u8212\'20\'14accustomed to haranguing 100 Senators over Supreme Court nominations rather than persuading millions of voters. But what a relief if America can look forward one day to the relative political peace Europe enjoys on this contentious moral question.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/europe-abortion-law-roe-v-wade-supreme-court-european-union-leak-alito-dobbs-pro-life-choice-civil-womens-rights-11651757568"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Europe's \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Lesson}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220508ei580035x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
