\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Biden at Center of Fight Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  After Career of Reservations; Democratic president, who previously called his position 'middle of the road,' is pressed by  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights supporters to be more outspoken in its defense against Republicans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey and Ken Thomas \par \uc2 1,403 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par 18:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14As a young senator, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he felt the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision that gave women the right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had gone too far. He briefly supported a constitutional amendment that would let states overturn the ruling.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 50 years later, President Biden is now serving as the de facto leader of the Democratic Party's efforts to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access following the recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion }}}\uc2  that would overturn the landmark ruling and empower many Republican-led states to outlaw abortions.\par \par \uc2 The injection of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics into the 2022 midterm campaign season gives Mr. Biden a cudgel to help rally Democrats seeking to maintain control of Congress in November's elections as the party contends with what had been a lack of enthusiasm among its voter base. It has also thrust the president into an issue he has long approached with reservations, and he has previously espoused more centrist views than many other Democrats. Before this week, he had not spoken the word "\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 " as president.\par \par \uc2 The White House hasn't said whether Mr. Biden will give an address soon on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or participate in any coming events planned by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters.\par \par \uc2 Following the leak, Mr. Biden called the potential decision "radical" and immediately sought to broaden his focus to talk about how such a ruling by the court's conservative justices could undermine privacy rights and affect issues beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Instead of focusing on the impact overturning Roe would have on women now, he repeatedly invoked his debates on privacy issues with the late Judge Robert Bork, whose Supreme Court nomination Mr. Biden helped scuttle in 1987 over Mr. Bork's opposition to a ruling that established that married couples could buy birth control.\par \par \uc2 "This is about a lot more than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mr. Biden said Wednesday. "What happens if states change the law saying that children who are LGBTQ can't be in classrooms with other children? Is that legit under the way the decision is written? What are the next things that are going to be attacked?\par \par \uc2 During his remarks Tuesday, Mr. Biden also drew attention when he referred to the decision to "abort a child," a phrase that is not used by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates and was quickly cited by Republicans as evidence that Mr. Biden believes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases involve a child, rather than a fetus.\par \par \uc2 "Intentionally or not, at least Biden is no longer using euphemisms," tweeted Sen. Tom Cotton (R., Ark.).\par \par \uc2 Asked about Mr. Biden's choice of words, press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said: "The president's view on a woman's right to make choices about her own healthcare is well known, well documented, well stated."\par \par \uc2 Some activists have made clear they want to hear a more vigorous defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from Mr. Biden. Following the leak of the draft opinion, published by Politico, crowds gathered outside the Supreme Court on Tuesday. At one point, those supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights started chanting, "Where is Joe? Where is Joe?"\par \par \uc2 "The president is absolutely missing in action," said Renee Bracey Sherman, the founder and executive director of We Testify, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group. "We've been asking for a speech for over a year. Every time there is a crisis in this country he makes a speech. We couldn't get him to say the word '\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' in the State of the Union."\par \par \uc2 The White House said it is weighing how to respond if Roe is overturned, but has offered few details ahead of a ruling later this spring. The White House's Gender Policy Council has been exploring potential responses, including ways to help women travel to states that allow legal abortions and mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related medications to those in need through Medicaid, according to a White House official.\par \par \uc2 But White House officials say that the overturning of Roe would leave them with few options to respond in the short term.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2  spokesman Sam Lau said: "The president said earlier this week that his administration is continuing with the whole-of-government response to this crisis. We need to continue to hear from them about those plans."\par \par \uc2 The White House hasn't said whether Mr. Biden intends to meet with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists or attend any demonstrations that are expected to take place in Washington and in large metropolitan areas around the nation next week. Ms. Psaki said Friday the administration is meeting with women's groups, activists and other elected officials.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's rhetoric about the leaked opinion has stood in contrast with other leaders in the Democratic Party, including Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At a fundraiser for Emily's List, which backs women candidates who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Ms. Harris accused Republican leaders of "trying to weaponize the use of the law against women. How dare they?"\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris is expected to weigh in more on the threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming weeks. During her commencement address Saturday at \uc2 Tennessee State University\uc2  in Nashville, Tenn., she said the U.S. was forced to defend principles such as "the rights of women to make decisions about their own bodies." \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
