\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Senators in Spotlight on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law as Roe v. Wade Teeters; With vote planned for next week, lawmakers face questions about restrictions, exceptions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lindsay Wise and Siobhan Hughes \par \uc2 1,109 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par 17:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14After decades of status quo on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the possible reversal }}}\uc2  of the Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-wade-abortion-supreme-court-explainer-11651601806"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v. Wade ruling }}}\uc2  is putting pressure on lawmakers to stake out clear positions on what degree of restrictions or access they would support.\par \par \uc2 Headed into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-leak-of-roe-opinion-parties-make-dueling-bets-on-power-of-abortion-issue-11651752003"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the midterm elections }}}\uc2 , both parties are trying to paint the other as out of step with mainstream voters, with most Democrats backing broad access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and most Republicans supporting tight rules. If Roe and a related precedent are overturned, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-state-abortion-laws-stand-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11651614828"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some states are set to ban }}}\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright and mandate harsh penalties for healthcare providers who perform the procedure, while others would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in line with current law.\par \par \uc2 The threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access also prompted protests in several cities and outside the Supreme Court building. Washington's Metropolitan Police Department said it had activated civil disturbance units throughout the weekend in response to expected demonstrations around the city. The U.S. Capitol police referred questions to the Supreme Court's force but said officials were working closely with other agencies to prepare for potential protests.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.) plans a vote Wednesday to open debate on Democrats' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -access bill, which supporters concede has no chance of passing. The Women's Health Protection Act would codify protections established by Roe for a woman seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, or later if medically necessary. Fetal viability occurs around 22 to 24 weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to see where everyone stands," he said Thursday. "It's the first time there's a vote like this in a very, very long time, when the actual rights are at stake, when it's not an abstract exercise."\par \par \uc2 Most Republicans, meanwhile, have long opposed the limits established by Roe and have cast Democrats as opposing any restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I'll tell you the Democrats are the radical ones here. They want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until birth," said Sen. Rick Scott (R., Fla.), the chair of Senate Republicans' campaign arm.\par \par \uc2 The issue moved front and center after Politico {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/supreme-court-abortion-draft-opinion-00029473"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published a leaked draft opinion }}}\uc2 , labeled as from a majority of the court, by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  from February that said the Roe decision should be overturned. It doesn't necessarily reflect {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-confirms-authenticity-of-leaked-draft-opinion-overruling-roe-v-wade-11651592725"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a final ruling }}}\uc2 , which is expected in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say the bill they are bringing to the Senate floor next week would keep in place rules consistent with the protections currently provided by Roe, including late-term abortions if a woman's life or health is in danger. They say Republicans espousing a hard line are out of step with most voters, citing polls that consistently show support for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-a-supreme-court-ruling-on-roe-v-wade-would-mean-for-women-11651614994"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This is about who decides\u8212\'20\'14a woman, her family, her faith, her doctor\u8212\'20\'14or politicians," said Sen. \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2  (D., Mich.).\par \par \uc2 A recent Wall Street Journal poll found that 55% of voters surveyed said they wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal in all or most cases, while 30% said it should be illegal except in cases involving rape or incest or when the life of the mother is in danger, and 11% said it should be illegal in all circumstances.\par \par \uc2 About two dozen states, including Texas and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigans-abortion-tensions-reflect-uncertain-future-in-battleground-states-11651834802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michigan }}}\uc2 , have laws on the books that could ban most abortions if Roe is overturned, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2  (R., N.D.), who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acknowledged the public might balk at some of the new state restrictions set to take effect should the high court overturn Roe. A trigger law in his home state would make performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a felony at any point in a pregnancy unless the life of the mother was in danger. There are no exceptions for victims of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cramer said he wasn't sure he would support jail time for doctors who perform abortions, and he wouldn't be surprised if the state legislature tweaks the law when it meets in January.\par \par \uc2 "We may see some tinkering with that, when the theory becomes a reality, right?" he said. "It's amazing how reality will do that for people."\par \par \uc2 Other Republicans saw little incentive to speculate about what the end of Roe will mean, since the court's ruling isn't final. "See, I don't think the goose is cooked yet," said Sen. \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2  (R., Ind.).\par \par \uc2 Ahead of next week's vote, Mr. Schumer said he wouldn't bring up an alternative bill backed by moderate Republican Sens. Lisa Murkowski of Alaska and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine, which aims to codify Roe while containing explicit exemptions for religious institutions and allowing states to impose some restrictions. "We are not looking to compromise something as vital as this," Mr. Schumer said.\par \par \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  Chairman Dick Durbin of Illinois, the No. 2 Senate Democrat, said that it might be possible to find common ground with Ms. Murkowski and Ms. Collins, the only two Republicans in the Senate who identify as supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Through a spokeswoman, Ms. Collins said she shares with Mr. Durbin a "common goal of wanting to put into federal law the major tenets" of Roe and a related \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling, Planned Parenthood v. Casey.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related legislation has repeatedly failed in the Senate, where 60 votes are required to advance most bills. In 2020, 53 senators voted for a bill to ban abortions starting at 20 weeks into a pregnancy, with Sens. Bob Casey (D., Pa.) and Joe Manchin (D., W.Va.) joining the Republicans who voted in favor and Ms. Collins and Ms. Murkowski joining the Democrats who voted against. A vote earlier this year to codify Roe also failed to advance, with Mr. Manchin joining Republicans in opposition.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans succeed in winning back control of Congress in November, some Republicans may want to pursue an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at the federal level, said Sen. \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2  (R., Mo.).\par \par \uc2 "There will be some of our members who will be interested in, I think, a federal approach, and others will be interested in letting the states do this, and I don't know how that coalesces," said Mr. Blunt. He said his own view is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy should be left to the states.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lindsay Wise at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:lindsay.wise@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lindsay.wise@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Siobhan Hughes at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:siobhan.hughes@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 siobhan.hughes@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/senators-in-spotlight-on-abortion-law-as-roe-v-wade-teeters-11651850655"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senators in Spotlight on \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law as Roe v. Wade Teeters  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220506ei56003ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
