\par \par \uc2 Ultrasounds from women served by Pregnancy Aid Detroit, a group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and provides free pregnancy testing and ultrasounds, in Detroit on Thursday. PHOTO: Sylvia Jarrus for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigan-governor-abortion-rights-groups-challenge-dormant-state-abortion-ban-11649345756"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a law passed in 1931 }}}\uc2  could automatically spring back into effect if Roe is overturned, making it a felony to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the life of the woman is in danger. The law, if revived, would immediately become one of the strictest in the country.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates in the state are pushing a ballot measure that would take the state in the opposite direction, making it one of the first in the nation to explicitly protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its constitution.\par \par \uc2 The state's Democratic governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , sued local prosecutors last month in an attempt to get the Michigan Supreme Court to declare the state's 1931 law unconstitutional. Planned Parenthood of Michigan and a local \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider filed a similar lawsuit the same day against the state's Democratic attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Harris, a professor of obstetrics and gynecology at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  who provides abortions, said doctors at her hospital are preparing for the possibility they will have to largely stop performing the procedure soon\u8212\'20\'14and discussing how they could add more beds in their birth center and more spots in their neonatal intensive care unit in case births go up.\par \par \uc2 Doctors are also readying themselves in case efforts to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeed and Michigan becomes a destination for patients from nearby states such as Ohio, Indiana and Missouri, she said.\par \par \uc2 "The uncertainty is actually deep and it goes in two different directions," Dr. Harris said.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups say they too view this as a potentially transformational moment. "Michigan state law is really one of the best in the nation to protect the unborn child," said Genevieve Marnon, legislative director of Right to Life of Michigan, referring to the 1931 law. "The only reason we cannot fully enforce that is because of Roe v. Wade."\par \par \uc2 Currently, abortions are relatively easy to obtain in Michigan up to about 24 weeks of pregnancy, the point of fetal viability\u8212\'20\'14reflecting the protection of the Supreme Court's 1973 Roe v. Wade decision enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Around 30 facilities provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, and on average women drive less than 20 miles to one. On a recent Monday, hours before the leak of the Supreme Court decision, the scene around several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in low-rise suburban buildings was peaceful, with no protesters or heavy security. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
