\par \par \uc2 Nancy Anter, director of the antiabortion Pregnancy Aid Detroit, arranges new and gently used baby clothes on Thursday in Detroit. PHOTO: Sylvia Jarrus for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Paul Long, president and chief executive of the Michigan Catholic Conference, the advocacy arm of the Catholic Church in the state, said the group is advocating for more funding so it can help women who carry their babies to full term. The Republican-controlled Legislature included funding in the most recent state budget that he said would have facilitated their work, but the governor vetoed it.\par \par \uc2 "Whoever the governor is, we've been out there advocating that these services are necessary for women," Mr. Long said.\par \par \uc2 State leaders are deadlocked on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least until November. While Ms. Whitmer opposes a state ban, Republicans in the Legislature have been supportive of restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Whitmer and state Attorney General Dana Nessel are both up for reelection and have made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights central to their campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Nessel has said that while she won't enforce the old state ban if Roe v. Wade falls, she can't dictate what local prosecutors do. The prospect of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Michigan "is just this overwhelming sadness to me," she said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal in her suburban Detroit home.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court overturns Roe v. Wade, what impact would that have on November's midterm elections? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Nessel's Republican opponent, Matthew DePerno, who was endorsed by Mr. Trump in the primary, in a tweet this week said he is hopeful Roe will be overturned, calling it "a great win for the unborn and for states' rights."\par \par \uc2 Macomb County Prosecutor Peter Lucido, a Republican named as a defendant in the governor's lawsuit, said prosecutors don't pick and choose which laws to enforce. "There's a duty to uphold the law whether we like it or not," he said. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
