\par \par \uc2 Dr. Halley Crissman collecting signatures aimed at protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Ypsilanti, Mich., last weekend. PHOTO: Sylvia Jarrus for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups are in the process of gathering some 425,000 signatures by mid-July for the ballot measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state's Constitution. A similar measure failed by more than 20 percentage points in 1972, shortly before the Supreme Court decided Roe v. Wade, but more recent polling data shows a narrow majority of state residents support at least some access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On a recent Sunday, Dr. Halley Crissman and Dr. Lauren Owens, advocacy co-chairs of the Michigan branch of the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , stood outside a farmers market in Ypsilanti, a liberal enclave near Ann Arbor, gathering signatures to get the amendment on the ballot. They ran out of space on their signature sheets before their three-hour shift was done.\par \par \uc2 "It feels like we're on the brink of losing access to most necessary, safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care," Dr. Crissman said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigans-abortion-tensions-reflect-uncertain-future-in-battleground-states-11651834802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michigan's \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Tensions Reflect Uncertain Future in Battleground States  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220506ei56000xe\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
