\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 After Leak of Roe v. Wade Opinion, Parties Make Dueling Bets on Power of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Issue; Democrats believe threat to  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights will boost party turnout; Republicans say economy, inflation remain voters' top concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aaron Zitner in Washington and Joshua Jamerson in Statesboro, Ga. \par \uc2 1,506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par 13:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats hope that the Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 draft opinion }}}\uc2  ending a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will accomplish what President Biden and his party in Congress so far haven't: Injecting urgency into the midterm elections so that Democrats feel the need to vote.\par \par \uc2 Republican leaders, by contrast, argue that the prospect of a long-sought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  victory will energize their own voters\u8212\'20\'14and that Democratic enthusiasm will continue to lag behind due to unhappiness with inflation, Covid-19 and what they portray as the majority party's limited legislative accomplishments in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Control of both chambers of Congress after November's elections could be determined by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-supreme-court-abortion-opinion-leak-parties-weigh-next-steps-11651656601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which party's analysis of the \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  issue }}}\uc2  is right.\par \par \uc2 Democrat Jill Shaw, a Kentucky native who retired to Florida, said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hunt-for-who-leaked-draft-roe-v-wade-opinion-has-no-road-map-11651748254"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the expected court ruling }}}\uc2  gives her extra motivation to vote this year and to encourage others to do so.\par \par \uc2 "This will definitely be front and center for me as an issue," said Ms. Shaw, 66, who considers herself a moderate Democrat and has friends who had abortions when they were younger. "I just can't believe the next generation isn't going to have that choice. It's important that all women step up."\par \par \uc2 By contrast, David Brasington, a Republican and retired building-maintenance worker from Statesboro, Ga., doubted that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling would outweigh the factors that have dispirited Democratic voters and would bring them to the polls. "With everything I've seen, Democrats are tired of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/why-gas-prices-expensive-11646767172"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high gas prices }}}\uc2 , too," said Mr. Brasington, 66 years old.\par \par \uc2 Speculation about the political impact of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling turns on a central fact about midterm elections: Voter turnout tends to drop among the president's party, while the opposing party, unhappy over losing the last presidential election, is eager to vote again.\par \par \uc2 That disparity is turning up in surveys such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.foxnews.com/official-polls/05-03-22-fox-news-poll-voters-are-most-worried-about-inflation-and-the-countrys-future"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a recent Fox News poll }}}\uc2 , which found that 52% of Republicans but only 41% of Democrats rated themselves as having the highest level of interest in the midterms\u8212\'20\'14an 11-point GOP advantage.\par \par \uc2 Now, the two parties are watching to see if voters react as they have predicted to the prospect of a Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade, the landmark decision that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-wade-abortion-supreme-court-explainer-11651601806"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 established \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  as a constitutional right }}}\uc2 . That possibility was strongly signaled in the draft ruling that became public this week, but the court's final decision isn't set.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic hope is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and the prospect of losing them, carry such emotional salience that the issue will resonate with the party's core supporters. Some analysts outside see that as the likely outcome, even if the court's final decision is less sweeping.\par \par \uc2 Christine Matthews, a pollster who has worked for Republican candidates, said focus groups she runs suggest that the issue carries impact. "I hear people saying that Roe being overturned seems abstract, that 'it couldn't happen in our state,' " she said. "The leak of the opinion is like a big bomb dropping\u8212\'20\'14it will wake people up and make them say, 'Oh, it could happen to me.' "\par \par \uc2 Others rebuffed the idea that news of the draft opinion fundamentally changes the political environment.\par \par \uc2 "I think the effect is going to be significantly less than anticipated,'' said Gene Ulm, a GOP pollster. States with the heaviest concentrations of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters also tend to have less-restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, and so impact there will be limited, he said. "The sky could fall but not hit anyone,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 "The things driving the electorate are inflation, the cost of gas\u8212\'20\'14the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/americans-are-showing-inflation-fatigue-and-some-companies-see-a-breaking-point-11651410002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pocketbook cluster of issues }}}\uc2 . Americans are under duress, and [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] is not in that category,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Polling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights gives a mixed picture of which voters would be most motivated to vote, or what choices they would make, as the result of a change in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights law.\par \par \uc2 A range of polling finds little appetite for the Supreme Court to overturn Roe. Surveys by CNN and the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  have found about 70% support for retaining Roe, and Gallup polls have pegged support at about 60%.\par \par \uc2 But a Wall Street Journal poll in March found plurality support, though narrowly, for laws that ban most abortions after the 15th week of pregnancy. That is a narrower window for legal abortions than under current Supreme Court rulings.\par \par \uc2 Gallup surveys and the Journal poll showed little sign that women hold different views than men, or that young voters are more supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights than older voters. The surveys didn't reveal, however, which groups found \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to be the issue that would be most important to their vote in November. Some Democrats contend that a decision overturning Roe would particularly boost turnout among women and young voters. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal7200
