\par \par \uc2 Cheri Whitney, a Republican from Georgia, would like to see Roe overturned. PHOTO: Joshua Jamerson/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 In a memo to GOP candidates, the Republicans' main Senate campaign arm suggested focusing on a set of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions that are popular, based on its polling of likely voters nationally and in battleground states. The memo, from the National Republican Senatorial Committee, said there was majority support for the idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ends a human life; for allowing states, rather than "unelected judges,'' to set \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy; and for banning late-term abortions and the use of tax money for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cheri Whitney, a Republican and store owner from Lake Park, Ga., celebrated the prospect that Roe would be struck down and credited former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/white-house-counsel-others-steer-trumps-supreme-court-nomination-strategy-11600553569"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appointment of three conservatives }}}\uc2  to the court. "I don't think the murder of children is something the United States should be doing," she said.\par \par \uc2 Rhonda Pierce Moreno, 58, a Democratic voter in Lake Oswego, Ore., said she was already changing her candidate choice in a local House primary election as a result of the leaked court opinion. Ms. Moreno, a retired opinions clerk at a federal court, said she had decided to back Democratic Rep. \uc2 Kurt Schrader\uc2  in his re-election campaign, rather than his more liberal opponent, who had been her initial choice. She said she worried that the more liberal candidate could lose in November.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Moreno said she feared that the Supreme Court would "destroy the rights of women in our country that we've had for nearly 50 years," and that she "realized more than ever that the Democrats need to keep control of Congress.''\par \par \uc2 Sara Jo Britt, 70, a Republican retired human resources worker from Jenkins County, Ga., said she didn't mind if individual states permitted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "If Georgia said no, and Florida said no, but California wanted to say yes\u8212\'20\'14you know, that's between the person and God," Ms. Britt said.\par \par \uc2 While many Republicans say that Americans are focused most on other issues, some strategists and pollsters say the emotional impact of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling could grow, if Democratic and swing voters come to fear that could lead to the erosion in other rights, such as same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 "Now Democrats have a sort of unifying message label, which is that Republicans are going to take us backwards on these issues,'' said Ms. Matthews, whose clients have included Republican Gov. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  of Maryland. "If they do that, it would be more powerful than just an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  message as a stand-alone.''\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court overturns Roe v. Wade, what impact would that have on November's midterm elections? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 One group in Georgia said it would start to connect the potential repeal of Roe to the possible rollback of other rights and policies, such as affirmative action or access to contraception. "It's a short walk to a number of other issues that folks can see the handwriting on the wall for," said Francys Johnson, a board member of the New Project Georgia Action Fund, which works to increase voter turnout and engagement among Black, Latino and other voter groups.\par \par \uc2 Ruy Teixeira, a Democratic demographer, argues that Democrats face midterm challenges in large part because they are too identified with liberal stances on immigration, policing and race that have alienated centrist voters. He said that while party leaders have broad public support for their call to retain Roe, they must still move to the center on other issues.\par \par \uc2 "Democrats would be foolish to assume that this in and of itself is a 'get out of jail free' card,'' he said of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.\par \par \uc2 John McCormick, Eliza Collins and Lindsay Wise contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Aaron Zitner at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:aaron.zitner@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aaron.zitner@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Joshua Jamerson at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-leak-of-roe-opinion-parties-make-dueling-bets-on-power-of-abortion-issue-11651752003"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 After Leak of Roe v. Wade Opinion, Parties Make Dueling Bets on Power of \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Issue }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220505ei55001gx\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
