\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Kentucky Legislature Overrides Governor's Veto, Passes  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Restrictions; State's two providers say they will have to stop providing abortions because they are unable to comply with the requirements immediately\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Jennifer Calfas \par \uc2 848 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2022\par 00:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Kentucky Legislature overrode its governor's veto and passed new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations Wednesday that local providers said would force them to cease offering the procedure immediately, potentially making Kentucky the first state in decades without legal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bill imposes additional reporting requirements on providers related in part to medication abortions and stipulates that they can't dispose of fetal remains as medical waste and must work with a funeral home to provide individual burial or cremation, among other provisions. The bill also bans most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy with an exception for the life or health of the mother, similar to a Mississippi law now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-in-landmark-mississippi-abortion-case-11638354602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 being weighed by the Supreme Court  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bill is an emergency measure, meaning it goes into effect immediately. The state's two providers said they would be unable to comply with some of the requirements right away and would stop offering the procedure in the state unless a court blocks the bill from being implemented. They said it was unclear how long it would take them to comply with the new law or if they would be able to.\par \par \uc2 Republicans control both chambers of the state's Legislature. Wednesday's votes in the House of Representatives was 76 to 21, and the Senate voted 31 to 6.\par \par \uc2 The bill first passed earlier this spring, but Democratic Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  said some provisions were likely unconstitutional and that the Legislature hadn't allocated the funding needed to implement many of the new requirements.\par \par \uc2 Democratic lawmakers in both chambers criticized the legislation Wednesday, calling it likely unconstitutional and harmful to women. "It's a slap in the face," said Rep. Mary Lou Marzian, a Democrat. "It's insulting to Kentucky women and their families."\par \par \uc2 Rep. Norma Kirk-McCormick, a Republican, called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  "plain wrong." "There's no mercy for that baby in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she added.\par \par \uc2 Proponents of the bill say it contains common-sense requirements to ensure the dignity of fetal remains and protect women's health by, for example, more closely tracking potential complications from medication abortions. Addia Wuchner, executive director of Kentucky Right to Life and a former Kentucky legislator, said politicians have spent years crafting the regulations and working with groups to ensure they can be implemented.\par \par \uc2 "There's no wording in there that would totally restrict or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Ms. Wucher said.\par \par \uc2 Providers said there is no way for them to comply with the law given its immediate effective date, including because they said some of the additional forms they need to file don't exist yet.\par \par \uc2 They said it would be difficult to comply with the law even long term because some of the information they are required to provide on the forms would violate patient privacy and they don't think they could find funeral homes who would work with them to dispose of remains.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky could become the first state in nearly five decades where women won't have access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any stage of pregnancy, according to \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2  , a state policy analyst at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics.\par \par \uc2 "We know the intent of this law is to shut us down," said Rebecca Gibron, interim chief executive of Planned Parenthood Great Northwest, Hawai'i, Alaska, Indiana, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma's legislature recently passed a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any stage of pregnancy, which the state's Republican governor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/oklahoma-legislature-passes-near-total-ban-on-abortion-11649192968"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed earlier this week  }}}\uc2 . It goes into effect later this year.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  said Wednesday they planned to file lawsuits in Kentucky federal court asking a court to freeze implementation of the law. They argue that the law violates women's federal constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability and that it violates the clinics due process rights by imposing criminal and civil penalties without giving them the means to comply with the new regulations.\par \par \uc2 The Kentucky legislature also has put a measure on the ballot in November that would amend the commonwealth's constitution to say that it doesn't protect a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kentucky is one of at least a handful of states where the governor and state legislature are divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, setting up pitched battles in the coming weeks before and after the Supreme Court is expected to issue a landmark decision on federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Michigan's Democratic governor last week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigan-governor-abortion-rights-groups-challenge-dormant-state-abortion-ban-11649345756"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a lawsuit  }}}\uc2  arguing that her state's own 91-year-old law banning most abortions violates the state constitution. The governor has tried unsuccessfully to get the state's Republican legislature to repeal the law.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jennifer.Calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jennifer.Calfas@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kentucky-legislature-overrides-governors-veto-passes-abortion-restrictions-11649896715"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kentucky Legislature Overrides Governor's Veto, Passes \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Restrictions  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220414ei4e0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
