\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Support for 15-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Outweighs Opposition, WSJ Poll Finds; Majority think  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most cases, but many back 15-week ban being considered by Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey \par \uc2 1,174 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par 14:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 The latest Wall Street Journal poll asked U.S. voters their opinions of 15-week and six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, with exemptions to protect the health of the mother. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said the poll asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans with exemptions to protect the life of the mother. (Corrected on April 1)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14More American voters favor the idea of a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban than oppose it, according to the latest Wall Street Journal poll, as the Supreme Court prepares to issue a ruling that could alter the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape.\par \par \uc2 With lawmakers in several states pushing forward with bills that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, 48% of voters said they would strongly or somewhat favor such restrictions, with exemptions to protect the health of the mother, while 43% were in opposition.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the survey found a majority of voters say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, underscoring the complicated views many Americans hold on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Holly Renaud, 32, of Pensacola, Fla., said she supported a ban at 15 weeks or earlier. "I think that the only real exception that should be made is when it comes down to health of the mother versus health of the child," she said.\par \par \uc2 Tosha Wise, 53, of New York City, said: "I'm on the fence with it."\par \par \uc2 "I understand where they are coming from, but I also think women should have the right," she said, adding: "God forbid, I should get raped.\u8230\'20\'26I should have that choice."\par \par \uc2 A total of 31 percent of voters said they strongly support a 15-week ban, while 17 percent were somewhat in support. Another 34 percent said they strongly opposed such a ban, and 10 percent said they were somewhat opposed.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-supreme-court-weighs-abortion-rights-more-states-look-at-15-week-bans-11645641664"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court is considering }}}\uc2  whether to allow a 15-week ban passed in Mississippi, which is at odds with the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision and other precedents that established a constitutional right to end a pregnancy. The high court has previously established the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until a fetus is able to sustain meaningful life outside of the womb, which generally occurs at weeks 22 to 24 of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Following Mississippi, lawmakers in Arizona and Florida also recently passed laws {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arizona-legislature-passes-15-week-abortion-ban-11648156791"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning most abortions after 15 weeks }}}\uc2  of pregnancy, and Arizona's governor signed his state's measure Wednesday. Similar bills in West Virginia and Kentucky have passed one chamber in those state legislatures. Officials who supported these bills say, if passed, the laws would allow states to more quickly and easily enforce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions should the Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-in-landmark-mississippi-abortion-case-11638354602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 uphold the 15-week ban. }}}\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi law and other measures like it are at odds with current Supreme Court precedent, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates worry that a win for the state could open the door to wider restrictions. Courts have traditionally blocked these more restrictive laws from being enforced.\par \par \uc2 Democratic lawmakers in other states have pushed for new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/new-jersey-adds-abortion-protections-to-state-law-11642102312"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -rights protections, }}}\uc2  while clinics in some of those states plan for an anticipated influx in patients coming in from out of state.\par \par \uc2 A report last year by the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2  and Prevention estimated that about 95% of abortions in the U.S. in 2019 took place by 15 weeks of gestation.\par \par \uc2 Overall, 55% of voters surveyed said they wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal in all or most cases, while 30% said it should be illegal except in cases involving rape, incest or when the life of the mother is in danger. Another 11% said it should be illegal in all circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Among Democrats, 21% supported a 15-week ban and 69% opposed, while among Republicans, 75% supported a 15-week ban and 20% opposed.\par \par \uc2 "There are going to be hardened people on both ends" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, said Tony Fabrizio, a Republican pollster whose firm conducted the poll with the firm of Democratic pollster John Anzalone. "But most people are somewhere in between and a lot of people pick and choose."\par \par \uc2 Joann Belmares, 59, of McKeesport, Pa., said she supported access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the first trimester of a pregnancy. "There has to be a valid reason" after 15 weeks, she said. "Has to be more than 'I just changed my mind.'"\par \par \uc2 share your thoughts\par \par \uc2 Would you support a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Respondents were less supportive of restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the early stages of pregnancy. Asked about laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks, with exemptions for the health of the mother, 50% were opposed and 42% in favor.\par \par \uc2 Texas last year enacted a six-week ban, and Idaho's governor recently signed a similar law. The six-week mark of pregnancy is before or shortly after many women learn they are pregnant. Texas has been able to maintain its ban, despite conflicting with Supreme Court precedent, because the state {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238?"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 structured the law }}}\uc2  in a novel way that makes it difficult to challenge in court.\par \par \uc2 Justin McDaniel, 27, of Cullman, Ala., said he would support bans at six and 15 weeks. He said that Roe v. Wade should be overturned, though he said, "I'm willing to concede certain exceptions for rape and incest."\par \par \uc2 Haley Swilling, 44, of Chicago said she supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in most cases. Of a 15-week ban, she said there need to be exceptions for rape survivors and expressed concerns about issues that could come up later in a pregnancy. "I would require more exceptions," she said.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Singleton, 42, of San Francisco, also said a 15-week ban would be too restrictive. She said: "I'm OK with some limitations. Once you get to the viability of the fetus, that to me is the cutoff."\par \par \uc2 "I'm adopted," said Ms. Singleton. "I've really struggled as a woman who is adopted. I still come down to a woman should have the right to control her body."\par \par \uc2 Molly Murphy, a Democratic pollster who worked on the Journal poll, said the range of responses "speaks to the conflict in people's minds on this issue." She added: "Most people want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal, but where you draw the line varies."\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal poll surveyed 1,500 registered voters from March 2 through March 7. The margin of error for the question about whether voters wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal or illegal was plus or minus 2.5 percentage points. The margin of error for the questions about 15-week and six-week bans was plus or minus 3.6 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Zitner and Laura Kusisto contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Catherine Lucey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:catherine.lucey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catherine.lucey@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/support-for-15-week-abortion-ban-outweighs-opposition-wsj-poll-finds-11648821601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Support for 15-Week \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ban Outweighs Opposition, WSJ Poll Finds  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220401ei41002gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
