\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Arizona Legislature Passes 15-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban; Bill comes ahead of pivotal Supreme Court decision that could alter  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights landscape nationwide\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas \par \uc2 750 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 March 2022\par 21:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Arizona lawmakers passed a bill banning most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, becoming the second state legislature to push through this restriction ahead of a pivotal Supreme Court decision that could alter the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights landscape nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The Arizona House of Representatives passed the bill in a 31-26 vote Thursday. It now heads to the desk of Arizona Gov. \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  , a Republican who has signed restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since becoming governor in 2015.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Mr. Ducey didn't immediately respond to a request for comment Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The legislative efforts in Arizona follow similar moves in Florida, where Republican lawmakers earlier this month {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/florida-senate-passes-15-week-abortion-ban-11646409503"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approved a ban on most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These bills are similar to the Mississippi law at the center of a Supreme Court case that challenges the high court's 1973 Roe v. Wade decision, which established a constitutional right to end a pregnancy. Similar bills in West Virginia and Kentucky passed one chamber in those state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 The Arizona law, known as SB 1164, bars abortions in the state after 15 weeks of pregnancy, weeks earlier than current precedent established by the Supreme Court. The law allows for exceptions in medical emergencies. It doesn't include exceptions for instances of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers who supported these bills say, if passed, the laws {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-supreme-court-weighs-abortion-rights-more-states-look-at-15-week-bans-11645641664"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 would allow states to more quickly and easily  }}}\uc2  enforce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions similar to Mississippi's should the Supreme Court uphold the 15-week ban. Some Republican state lawmakers said they anticipate the court could either uphold this law or overturn Roe v. Wade. A decision is expected no later than the summer.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Rep. John Kavanagh, one of the Republican lawmakers who introduced the 15-week ban and voted in favor of the bill Thursday, told The Wall Street Journal earlier this year he wished to prepare the state for either of those outcomes. Arizona is one of several states with pre-existing laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that were passed before Roe v. Wade. If the high court's precedent is overturned, the law could be resurrected.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates say the 15-week ban is unconstitutional and would be harmful to people who can get pregnant and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Advocates also say they worry this law would open the door for harsher restrictions in the future.\par \par \uc2 "Make no mistake\u8212\'20\'14the legislators who voted in favor of this law in the House and Senate will not stop at a 15-week ban," said Brittany Fonteno, president of Planned Parenthood Advocates of Arizona, in a statement. "They will continue to chip away at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care until \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal in Arizona."\par \par \uc2 Data from the \uc2 Arizona Department of Health Services\uc2  showed about 5% of the state's abortions in 2020 happened after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 States aiming to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in recent years have tried to enact bans earlier into pregnancy than what is currently allowed under Supreme Court precedent. The high court has previously established the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until a fetus is able to sustain meaningful life outside of the womb, which falls around 24 weeks of pregnancy. Courts have blocked these more restrictive laws from being enforced.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Heartbeat Act, which bans abortions after six weeks of pregnancy in the Lone Star State, is an exception. The law, which took effect in September 2021, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 created a new enforcement structure  }}}\uc2  that allows private citizens to bring civil lawsuits against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers as well as anyone who aids or abets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Idaho Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  , a Republican, signed a similar bill on Wednesday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/idaho-governor-signs-six-week-abortion-ban-based-on-texas-model-but-voices-concerns-11648071555"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning abortions after about six weeks  }}}\uc2  of pregnancy. The legislation doesn't grant officials enforcement authority and instead gives that power to private citizens\u8212\'20\'14a precedent the governor expressed concerns over.\par \par \uc2 Democratic lawmakers in other states have pushed for new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protections, while clinics in some of those states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/illinois-abortion-clinics-are-preparing-for-a-potential-post-roe-world-11638419374"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 plan for an anticipated influx in patients  }}}\uc2  coming in from out of state. New Jersey joined more than a dozen other states in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/new-jersey-adds-abortion-protections-to-state-law-11642102312"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 establishing a right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  and other reproductive healthcare under state statute. Voters in Vermont {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/vermont-is-one-step-closer-to-adding-abortion-rights-to-its-constitution-11644348231"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will consider a constitutional amendment  }}}\uc2 in November establishing a right to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arizona-legislature-passes-15-week-abortion-ban-11648156791"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona Legislature Passes 15-Week \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ban  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220324ei3o0060p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
