\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Idaho Governor Signs Six-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Based on Texas Model, but Voices Concerns; Republican says law sets dangerous precedent by 'deputizing private citizens' to enforce it through private lawsuits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 856 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2022\par 21:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Idaho Republican Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  on Wednesday signed into law a bill that bans abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy and vests enforcement powers with private citizens instead of government officials, even as he expressed reservations about the legislation.\par \par \uc2 With the governor's signature, Idaho becomes the first state to follow {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/court-ruling-deals-near-final-blow-to-suit-challenging-texas-abortion-law-11647031457"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a novel Texas law  }}}\uc2  that severely curtailed abortions in that state.\par \par \uc2 The Idaho law, which was passed by the Legislature earlier this month, gives family members of what the state describes as the "preborn child" the right to sue medical providers who provide abortions past the point at which cardiac activity is detected in an embryo. That is typically around the six-week mark of pregnancy, before or shortly after many women learn they are pregnant. It allows the plaintiff to recover damages of at least $20,000 plus legal fees. The law makes exceptions for rape, incest and medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 In signing the law, Mr. Little said, "I stand in solidarity with all Idahoans who seek to protect the lives of preborn babies." But his two-page statement also said the measure set a dangerous precedent and could prove to be "both unconstitutional and unwise."\par \par \uc2 "Deputizing private citizens to levy hefty monetary fines on the exercise of a disfavored but judicially recognized constitutional right for the purpose of evading court review undermines our constitutional form of government and weakens our collective liberties," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Little said the law could prompt other states to use similar tactics to pass legislation limiting gun rights and religious freedom. He also said he was concerned that the challenges of obtaining a police report could render the exception for rape and incest essentially meaningless.\par \par \uc2 Unless the law is blocked by the courts, it is set to go into effect in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do you think the Idaho law banning most abortions is constitutional? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Mistie DelliCarpini-Tolman, Idaho state director of Planned Parenthood Alliance Advocates, said she expects the bill to curtail most abortions in the state. The governor "is still responsible for putting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of reach for most Idahoans, and it's a shame that he listened to the extremists instead of standing up for survivors of rape and incest," she said.\par \par \uc2 Republican-led states around the country are moving to tighten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, prompted by the success of the Texas law and the prospect that the Supreme Court in the coming months could issue a decision weakening or eliminating constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-in-landmark-mississippi-abortion-case-11638354602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a case from Mississippi  }}}\uc2 . In that case, the justices are considering a state law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, roughly two months earlier than current court precedent allows.\par \par \uc2 At least a handful of states are considering similar provisions to the Texas law allowing private citizens to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, a new approach that has thus far evaded a successful constitutional challenge, even though a six-week ban is at odds with current constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Typically, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights litigants sue state officials in court and ask a judge to block those officials from enforcing restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . By giving no state officials any enforcement authority, Texas has short-circuited that legal strategy.\par \par \uc2 Similar Texas-style laws have been moving through the Oklahoma and Tennessee legislatures. A proposed Missouri law would give enforcement power to citizens against those who assist women in obtaining abortions in or out of the state.\par \par \uc2 In December, the Supreme Court left the Texas law in effect, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics couldn't sue certain state officials, including the Texas attorney general. Texas's highest court ruled this month that providers also can't sue state licensing officials, blocking the plaintiffs' last clear path to challenging the law.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law has already caused the number of abortions in the state to drop sharply, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-prompts-women-to-seek-out-of-state-clinics-11632315616"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 many patients have traveled  }}}\uc2  to neighboring Louisiana, Oklahoma and beyond to seek the procedure.\par \par \uc2 "That is one a lot of pro-life states are watching [to see] if there's a mechanism we can put in place" to stop abortions right away, said Sue Swayze Liebel, state policy director for the Susan B. Anthony List, an advocacy group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Idaho law differs in some ways from the Texas law, which allowed anyone and not just family members to sue and set the floor for damages at $10,000.\par \par \uc2 Idaho, Oklahoma and other states have learned from Texas and crafted their laws to be even more difficult to challenge by being more explicit that state officials can play no role, said \uc2 Jessica Arons\uc2  , a lawyer with \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "Texas provided other states with a road map to do it even more effectively," Ms. Arons said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/idaho-governor-signs-six-week-abortion-ban-based-on-texas-model-but-voices-concerns-11648071555"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Idaho Governor Signs Six-Week \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ban Based on Texas Model, but Voices Concerns  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220323ei3n004mq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
