\par \par \uc2 The Texas Heartbeat Act, also known as SB 8, was passed into law last year by the Republican-controlled Texas Legislature. PHOTO: Ilana Panich-Linsman for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 A ruling by Texas' highest court on Friday has left state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics without any clear legal path for broadly challenging the state's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Supreme Court ruled that state medical licensing officials play no role in the enforcement of the Texas Heartbeat Act, known as SB 8, which bars abortions as soon as cardiac activity is detected in an embryo.\par \par \uc2 The ruling, although technical, was a major blow to the clinics and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups because it left them no state officials to sue in their legal bid to block the nation's toughest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 For more than six months since the law took effect in September, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights challengers have struggled to find a way to block SB 8, even though the law is at odds with constitutional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections established by the Supreme Court's 1973 Roe v. Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 Unlike other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans enacted and struck down elsewhere, SB 8 gives the state government no explicit enforcement role. Instead, it deputizes the public, empowering anyone in the country to file lawsuits and seek monetary damages against clinics and their insurers or anyone helping a patient get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The law's private enforcement scheme has severely limited abortions in Texas\u8212\'20\'14driving many women to travel outside the state for the procedure\u8212\'20\'14and effectively prevented abortions-rights groups from mounting a case against state government officials to secure a court injunction against SB 8.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  in December already hobbled the lawsuit, brought by Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics represented by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The court's conservative majority left the law in effect and said the clinics couldn't sue certain state officials, including the Texas attorney general.\par \par \uc2 The court, though, did leave the clinics a narrow opening to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rules-abortion-clinics-can-challenge-texas-abortion-law-11639149404"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 potentially proceed with claims  }}}\uc2  against the head of the Texas Medical Board and other state licensing officials who could play some secondary part in SB 8's enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Friday's state-court ruling appeared to foreclose that possibility.\par \par \uc2 "We conclude that Texas law does not authorize the state-agency executives to enforce the Act's requirements, either directly or indirectly," Texas Supreme Court Justice Jeffrey S. Boyd wrote in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.txcourts.gov/media/1453763/220033.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 23-page opinion  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Marc Hearron of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the lead lawyer on the challenge, said that ruling effectively wipes out the lawsuit and leaves no end in sight for the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 "We don't have a basis to continue," he said.\par \par \uc2 In a tweet, Texas Republican Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  called the ruling a major victory. "The pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plaintiffs' lawsuit against the state is essentially finished," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Separately, more than a dozen lawsuits challenging SB 8 have been filed in Texas state court and could take months to sort out. So far as the litigation has played out, Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics have said they intend to comply with the law.\par \par \uc2 The legal battle over SB 8 has unfolded while the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  in a separate case is considering whether to reduce or eliminate the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established in Roe. A decision in that case, involving a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Mississippi, is expected by late June.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jacob Gershman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jacob.gershman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jacob.gershman@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/court-ruling-deals-near-final-blow-to-suit-challenging-texas-abortion-law-11647031457"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Court Ruling Deals Near-Final Blow to Suit Challenging Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220311ei3b006bt\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
