\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  Supports Government in Blocking Torture Evidence; Justices find foreign-intelligence ties would be harmed by letting Guantanamo detainee pursue evidence of torture, rule Kentucky official can defend antiabortion law opposed by governor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 1,028 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2022\par 20:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  blocked Guantanamo detainee Abu Zubaydah's request to obtain evidence from former \uc2 Central Intelligence Agency\uc2  contractors who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-quizzes-government-ban-on-black-site-testimony-11633550536"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allegedly tortured him at a "black site"  }}}\uc2  in Poland, siding 6-3 with government arguments that permitting such testimony could jeopardize U.S. relationships with foreign intelligence services.\par \par \uc2 In a second decision Thursday, an 8-1 court revived a bid by Kentucky's Republican attorney general to continue defending a state law banning a common second-trimester \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  method, after a federal appeals court found the measure unconstitutional. In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mississippi-asks-supreme-court-to-end-roe-v-wade-abortion-rights-11626985810"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 separate case from Mississippi  }}}\uc2 , the court currently is weighing whether to restrict or eliminate federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights altogether, with a decision expected before July.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zubaydah, who has been held at the Guantanamo military prison since 2006, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB1017669743211969760"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was captured in Pakistan in 2002  }}}\uc2 . Under the mistaken belief that he was a top al Qaeda commander, the CIA transferred him through secret overseas facilities where he was waterboarded, buried alive, given forced enemas and otherwise brutalized in a futile effort to extract information about future attacks.\par \par \uc2 "The Government has since concluded that this treatment constituted torture," {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/20-827_i426.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thursday's decision observed  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Court of Human Rights\uc2  found in 2014 that Mr. Zubaydah had been tortured in Poland, leading to an investigation by Polish prosecutors into the possible culpability of their country's officials. At the Polish prosecutors' request, Mr. Zubaydah sought evidence from two former CIA contractors, psychologists James Mitchell and John Jessen, who designed and executed what once were called "enhanced interrogation techniques."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  moved to block that request under a rule that halts civil litigation when "state secrets" could be exposed. A federal appeals court in San Francisco concluded there were some questions the interrogators could answer that wouldn't endanger national security.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  on Thursday found otherwise. Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  wrote for a majority that included most of the court's conservatives. Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  , usually part of the conservative majority, filed an impassioned dissent joined by Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  asserting that the government's true motive wasn't protecting intelligence but rather suppressing news of its own conduct. Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  filed a separate dissent.\par \par \uc2 The decision left open the possibility that a more narrowly framed evidence request could be permissible. Joe Margulies, an attorney for Mr. Zubaydah, said he would try.\par \par \uc2 "We are pleased the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  confirmed that the \uc2 United States Government\uc2  tortured Abu Zubaydah and agrees there is a pathway for him to finally uncover the truth about what happened to him at the hands of the CIA during a critical part of his detention," Mr. Margulies said.\par \par \uc2 Justice Breyer wrote that the information Mr. Zubaydah sought already was widely available, so the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/psychologist-defends-interrogations-of-sept-11-suspects-11579651450"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interrogators could themselves add little  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He credited instead a declaration filed by then-CIA Director \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  suggesting the agency's dealings with foreign officials could be jeopardized if the government's former contractors confirmed activities in Poland.\par \par \uc2 National security is protected when the CIA honors promises of secrecy "despite the passage of time, the existence of media reports, and changes in public opinion," Justice Breyer wrote.\par \par \uc2 "Obviously the Court condones neither terrorism nor torture, but in this case we are required to decide only a narrow evidentiary dispute," he added. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  , \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  joined all or part of the Breyer opinion.\par \par \uc2 In dissent, Justice Gorsuch accused the majority of willful blindness.\par \par \uc2 "There comes a point where we should not be ignorant as judges of what we know to be true as citizens," he wrote. "Ending this suit may shield the government from some further modest measure of embarrassment. But respectfully, we should not pretend it will safeguard any secret."\par \par \uc2 Justice Gorsuch recounted the vast public record on Mr. Zubaydah's treatment.\par \par \uc2 "As embarrassing as these facts may be, there is no state secret here," Justice Gorsuch wrote. "We should not let shame obscure our vision." (U.S. v. Zubaydah)\par \par \uc2 The Kentucky case, Cameron v. EMW Women's Surgical Center, involved the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-considers-reviving-appeal-of-kentucky-abortion-law-11634065600"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state's 2018 ban on dilation and evacuation  }}}\uc2 , an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure used for most women seeking to end second-trimester pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 A Louisville, Ky., \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic sued to invalidate the law, and lower courts agreed it violated current \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  precedent protecting women's rights to end pregnancies before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 After a 2020 ruling from the federal circuit court in Cincinnati, the Democratic administration of Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  declined to pursue further appeals. The state's Republican attorney general, \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2  , sought to enter the case to continue litigation, but the appeals court turned him down.\par \par \uc2 Writing for Thursday's majority, Justice Alito said the state's interest in defending its laws tilted toward allowing Mr. Cameron's intervention.\par \par \uc2 "The importance of ensuring that States have a fair opportunity to defend their laws in federal court has been recognized by Congress," Justice Alito wrote, joined by Chief Justice Roberts and Justices Thomas, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kagan, joined by Justice Breyer, agreed on separate grounds.\par \par \uc2 Justice Sotomayor dissented, arguing that the previous attorney general\u8212\'20\'14who happened to be Mr. Beshear\u8212\'20\'14had taken legal positions withdrawing from the case.\par \par \uc2 "Elections have consequences not just for the public but also for state officers who may find themselves bound by strategic litigation choices made by their predecessors," she wrote.\par \par \uc2 More on the \uc2 Supreme Court\par \par \uc2 Read the latest stories on the high court, selected by WSJ editors\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/hearings-for-judge-ketanji-brown-jackson-to-start-march-21-senate-democrats-say-11646245038"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hearings for Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson to Start March 21, Senate Democrats Say  }}}\uc2  (March 2)\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-to-hear-case-that-could-hobble-bidens-global-warming-approach-11645970402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court to Hear Case That Could Hobble Biden's Global Warming Approach  }}}\uc2  (Feb. 27)\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-supreme-court-weighs-abortion-rights-more-states-look-at-15-week-bans-11645641664"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As Supreme Court Weighs \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rights, More States Look at 15-Week Bans  }}}\uc2  (Feb. 24)\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-mulls-whether-states-can-revive-trump-immigration-policies-11645660523"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Mulls Arizona Bid to Revive Trump Immigration Policy  }}}\uc2  (Feb. 23)\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-supports-government-in-blocking-torture-evidence-11646337851"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Supports Government in Blocking Torture Evidence  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220303ei33005mt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
