\par\par \uc2 Latin America News\par \uc2 World\par \b \uc2 Colombia's Highest Court Decriminalizes  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  in Historic Shift; Country joins Argentina and Mexico in expanding  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights in a heavily Catholic region\b0\par\par\uc2 By Juan Forero \par \uc2 888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2022\par 01:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Correction Colombia's Constitutional Court decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 24 weeks of pregnancy in a ruling Monday, and Argentine President Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez last year signed a bill allowing abortions in the first 14 weeks. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said months instead of weeks. (Corrected on Feb. 22)\par \par \uc2 BOGOT\u193\'00\'C1, Colombia\u8212\'20\'14The highest court in Colombia decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the 24th week of pregnancy, making the country the third large country in Latin American to lift stringent restrictions on the procedure in a little more than a year.\par \par \uc2 The country's Constitutional Court ruled 5-4 Monday in a case brought by a coalition of women's rights groups. The decision comes after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mexicos-supreme-court-decriminalizes-abortion-in-historic-shift-11631049288"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mexico's Supreme Court ruled last September }}}\uc2  that making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime was unconstitutional. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-once-unthinkable-in-argentina-becomes-legal-11609314445"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Argentina's Congress legalized \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  up to the 14th week of pregnancy in December 2020.\par \par \uc2 The shift in these countries energized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates across a region {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/why-the-catholic-church-is-losing-latin-america-11641914388"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 that is mostly Catholic or evangelical }}}\uc2  and whose religious leaders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/pope-francis-faces-tests-on-abortion-gay-rights-11632913201"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strongly oppose the procedure }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Outside the Constitutional Court in Bogot\u225\'00\'E1 late Monday afternoon, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters danced to reggaeton music, celebrating a victory that many had previously thought near-impossible to achieve in this conservative country of 50 million. The court ruled criminalizing selective abortions violated women's rights under the constitution.\par \par \uc2 "It's a historic decision that provides a wide opportunity for women and girls to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this country, the most ample in Latin America," said Catalina Martinez, the 37-year-old director of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a spokeswoman for Just Cause, the coalition of groups that brought the suit. "It is a convincing message for Latin America and the Caribbean."\par \par \uc2 By decriminalizing the procedure for the first 24 weeks of pregnancy, Colombia goes further than some European nations, including France, which allows abortions up to the 12th week of pregnancy. Colombia now rivals some U.S. states such as Florida and Pennsylvania, where abortions are also legal up to 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Catalina Calderon, director of the Women's Equality Center in the Colombian capital, said advocates for expanding access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  "have led Colombia to the most advanced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation in Latin America, and one of the most advanced in the world."\par \par \uc2 Many people in Colombia had battled against expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Natalia Bernal, a lawyer who has advocated for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, called the ruling "a fatal decision for Colombia."\par \par \uc2 "It's one of the cruelest court decisions against children's rights," she said. "It's not just against women but against the unborn. There are long-term consequences."\par \par \uc2 In Colombia, as in many countries where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been restricted, affluent and urban women have for years been able to more easily obtain the procedure than poor women in the countryside who are dependent on public hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Still, health authorities have estimated that 400,000 women terminate pregnancies annually here, the vast majority illegally, often using drugs such as misoprostol, which is used to treat ulcers and induce labor.\par \par \uc2 While the campaign to broaden access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/colombia-weighs-legalizing-abortion-a-first-for-big-latin-american-countries-11583102273"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 energized young people in Colombia }}}\uc2 , millions of people here remain opposed to making the procedure selectively available. In a recent poll by Cifras y Conceptos, 59% of respondents said they didn't agree that the Constitutional Court should decriminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all circumstances so as to not discriminate against women. Still, respondents opposed sending women to jail for seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 Until now, Colombia {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/colombia-upholds-abortion-laws-as-legalization-battle-shifts-to-argentina-11583203155"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had been like many other countries in the region }}}\uc2 , permitting the procedure only in cases of rape, when the mother's life is in danger or because of a malformation of the fetus. Two years ago, elective abortions in Latin America were permitted only in three small countries: Uruguay, Cuba and Guyana\u8212\'20\'14along with Mexico City. The procedure was banned in all instances in several others, including El Salvador, where women have been jailed when discovered to have had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, in January 2021, Argentine President Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez signed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill that Congress had been approved the previous month, allowing terminations in the first 14 weeks. Eight months later, Mexico's Supreme Court paved the way for far greater access to legal abortions, striking down provisions of law in two northern states that outlawed selective abortions. Justices said their ruling would establish a precedent applying to all states in Mexico, a country of 127 million.\par \par \uc2 Momentum is building in other Latin American countries toward expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/chiles-presidential-election-pits-young-versus-old-in-key-vote-for-economy-11639908003"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In Chile, }}}\uc2  President-elect Gabriel Boric has said he supports easing restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And members of a special assembly who are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/chile-elects-left-leaning-assembly-to-replace-dictatorship-era-constitution-11621270721"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writing a new constitution }}}\uc2  took the first step to expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access after a committee recently approved a proposal that would make the procedure a right under the nation's new charter. Currently, abortions are legal in Chile only when a pregnancy results from rape, when the fetus is unlikely to survive or if a woman's life is at risk.\par \par \uc2 Jenny Carolina Gonzalez in Bogot\u225\'00\'E1 and Ryan Dube in Lima, Peru, contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Juan Forero at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Juan.Forero@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Juan.Forero@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/colombias-highest-court-decriminalizes-abortion-in-historic-shift-11645490007"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colombia's Highest Court Decriminalizes \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  in Historic Shift }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220222ei2m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
