\par\par \uc2 China News\par \uc2 World\par \b \uc2 China Tries to Dial Back Its High  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rate; A 'campaign of intervention' signals a shift in attitude toward a much-used procedure as birthrates drop\b0\par\par\uc2 By Liyan Qi \par \uc2 930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2022\par 16:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 China is stepping up efforts to reduce abortions in young women, signaling a shift in official attitude toward a widely used procedure that has long been part of state-directed family planning.\par \par \uc2 A recent plan by the government-backed China Family-Planning Association outlined a "campaign of intervention" to reduce unplanned pregnancies and abortions among adolescents and unmarried women. It said the aim was to "improve \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The plan, posted on the association's website, was dated Jan. 28 and has been referred to in some Chinese media in recent days.\par \par \uc2 The wording in the plan comes as China faces rapidly declining births. China now allows couples to have three children, a 180-degree policy shift from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/china-is-haunted-by-its-one-child-policy-as-it-tries-to-encourage-couples-to-conceive-11641205807"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the decadeslong one-child policy }}}\uc2 , one legacy of which is a dwindling number of women of childbearing age. Over the past few years, China has been closing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and expanding services to help couples conceive.\par \par \uc2 The plan stressed it was important to "reshape the parenting culture of multichild families."\par \par \uc2 The plan isn't a government directive, but reflects government priorities for how to lift birth numbers. The language about intervention to reduce abortions was stronger than rhetoric used by Beijing previously. A plan issued by \uc2 China's State Council\uc2 , its top government body, in September, for example, included a goal to reduce "medically unnecessary" abortions, but didn't mention specific actions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in China and has been used heavily\u8212\'20\'14often as a way to comply with birth restrictions. In 1991, a time of some of the most brutal enforcement methods of the one-child policy, around 14 million abortions were performed in China, according to National Health Commission data. The number was just below nine million in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Some researchers say the widespread use of abortions may play a role in China's comparatively high rate of infertility, which according to a recent survey by \uc2 Peking University\uc2  researchers affects about 18% of couples of reproductive age, compared with a global average of around 15%.\par \par \uc2 Chinese demographers said the plan reflected an acknowledgment that the wide use of abortions, with many young women having repeat procedures, had harmful effects on fertility and that the plan shouldn't be seen as a precursor to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 The association's plan could mean measures such as promoting sex education, cracking down on illegal clinics and making contraceptive tools more available to young people, said Huang Wenzheng, a researcher at the Center for Globalization, a Beijing-based private think tank.\par \par \uc2 Neither the China Family-Planning Association nor the National Health Commission responded to requests for comments.\par \par \uc2 Even critics of China's birth policies welcomed the effort to reduce abortions. Yi Fuxian, a U.S.-based reproductive scientist who says China's decades of birth restrictions will distort demographic patterns for years to come, said that officials have good reasons to curb China's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, which are much higher than in most Asian countries. In 2020, a year when China had 12 million live births, it recorded nearly nine million abortions, according to official data.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the wording in the association's plan added to concerns among human-rights activists and Chinese feminists that abortions would become harder to access for Chinese women.\par \par \uc2 Yaqiu Wang, senior China researcher at Human Rights Watch, said that while it is unclear how the goal to reduce abortions will be implemented, "given the government's history of restricting women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  through abusive\u8212\'20\'14and sometimes violent\u8212\'20\'14means, this is certainly a cause for concern."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Mr. Yi said what is missing from Chinese birth policies\u8212\'20\'14even as China encourages births\u8212\'20\'14is a moral system that values humanity rather than economic or political goals. "The key for China is to respect life," Mr. Yi said.\par \par \uc2 It was unclear from the family-planning association's plan what alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be available to unmarried women who become pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Few concessions are made for births outside marriage in China. Most public hospitals require pregnant women to provide marriage certificates to receive routine pregnancy checkups or deliver their baby, in effect leaving a choice of paying for private care, getting married or terminating the pregnancy. Single mothers are often denied benefits such as paid maternity leave.\par \par \uc2 Chinese regulations also prohibit unmarried women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/a-single-woman-fights-to-freeze-her-eggs-in-china-11577106578"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 from freezing their eggs }}}\uc2 unless they have a health reason, such as cancer.\par \par \uc2 The China Family-Planning Association is a member of the \uc2 International Planned Parenthood Federation\uc2 , which also counts the \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2  as its member and which according to its website is "delivering sexual and reproductive healthcare around the world" and "fighting for sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Neither the international federation nor Planned Parenthood in the U.S. responded to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Wang Pei'an, a former top official in China's family-planning bureaucracy, which was created to enforce birth restrictions, is now the top Communist Party official at the Family-Planning Association. Mr. Wang is urging young people to be more responsible and have children.\par \par \uc2 "The increasingly serious adolescent sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  problems not only seriously damage the physical and mental health of adolescents and affect family happiness, but also pose a major threat to national population security and future national competitiveness," Mr. Wang told state media in July.\par \par \uc2 Write to Liyan Qi at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:liyan.qi@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 liyan.qi@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/china-tries-to-dial-back-its-high-abortion-rate-11644597797"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China Tries to Dial Back Its High \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rate }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220211ei2b004bl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
