\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Permits Continued Enforcement of Texas Six-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban; Court denies  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  providers' request to allow challenge to proceed in federal court; Justice Sotomayor's dissent calls case 'disaster for the rule of law'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 966 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2022\par 00:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  oversees the federal judiciary's New Orleans-based Fifth Circuit. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said Justice Neal Gorsuch did so. (Corrected on Jan. 20.)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Supreme Court allowed Texas' {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-governor-signs-fetal-heartbeat-abortion-ban-11621452346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban  }}}\uc2 to remain in effect indefinitely, issuing an order Thursday blocking litigation against the law while the state's highest court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-could-stay-in-effect-for-months-after-u-s-appeals-court-ruling-11642524651"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 weighs a procedural question  }}}\uc2 related to the measure's enforcement.\par \par \uc2 The court's order was unsigned and, as is typical, provided no explanation. The three liberal justices dissented, arguing that Thursday's order undermined {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rules-abortion-clinics-can-challenge-texas-abortion-law-11639149404"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the court's December decision  }}}\uc2  permitting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to proceed in limited fashion with their lawsuit against the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 "This case is a disaster for the rule of law and a grave disservice to women in Texas, who have a right to control their own bodies," Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote in dissent, joined by Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  and \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "With today's ruling, the lawsuit will continue in the appropriate venue, and the Texas Heartbeat Act will continue to save preborn lives," Texas Right to Life, a leading antiabortion group in the state, wrote {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://texasrighttolife.com/texas-heartbeat-act-lawsuit-to-stay-in-supreme-court-of-texas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in a statement  }}}\uc2  on its website.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, known as SB 8, bans nearly all abortions after about the sixth week of pregnancy, a restriction at odds with current Supreme Court precedent allowing women to obtain abortions prior to fetal viability, which occurs at about 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Texans have been without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for almost five months now, and there is no end in sight because the Supreme Court has done nothing to stop this unconstitutional ban," said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2  , president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents challengers to SB 8. "Seven other states have already introduced copycat bans now that the court has let Texas get away with this ploy."\par \par \uc2 The Texas attorney general's office didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 When states have enacted straightforward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, providers have been able to obtain injunctions by suing officials responsible for enforcement. Texas lawmakers sought to avoid such challenges by leaving enforcement up to private parties, providing them awards of at least $10,000 for filing civil lawsuits against those who provide abortions or assist women in obtaining them.\par \par \uc2 In its December decision, the Supreme Court by a 5-4 vote blocked a full-scale challenge to SB 8, finding that court clerks who would process SB 8 lawsuits weren't appropriate defendants. But by an 8-1 vote the court found that some licensing officials, such as the head of the state medical board, played a role in SB 8's enforcement and therefore could be sued.\par \par \uc2 At the providers' request, Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  expedited return of the case to the appeals court. In turn, the Fifth Circuit, rather than sending the case back to federal district court for proceedings against the licensing officials, scheduled argument on Texas' motion to refer to the state Supreme Court the question of whether the state licensing officials actually were involved in SB 8's enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Providers then returned to the Supreme Court on Jan. 3 asking the justices to order the Fifth Circuit to immediately transfer the case back to the district court. The clinics said the Fifth Circuit was entertaining superfluous legal questions that already had been resolved by the Supreme Court, delaying their opportunity to restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  took no action before the Fifth Circuit's Jan. 7 hearing, and the appeals court later granted Texas's request to send the issue to the state Supreme Court. That leaves SB 8 in effect at least until the Texas Supreme Court responds to the Fifth Circuit.\par \par \uc2 At a Jan. 7 hearing, at least one Fifth Circuit judge suggested that delay could be for the best: In a separate case from Mississippi, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is considering whether to reduce or eliminate the constitutional right to end unwanted pregnancies that it recognized in 1973 in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe we should just sit on this until the end of June and leave the hot potato with the Supreme Court," Judge Edith Jones said, referring to when the justices typically announce their most contentious decisions.\par \par \uc2 In a separate dissent joined by the other two liberals, Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  said the Fifth Circuit "ignored our judgment," leaving "an unconstitutional 6-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in effect in Texas\u8212\'20\'14as it has for over four months."\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, passed in May, bars doctors from knowingly performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is a "detectable fetal heartbeat," defined to include early cardiac activity in the embryo. It contains no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit blocked challenges to SB 8 before it took effect on Sept. 1; the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  , by a 5-4 vote, declined then to intercede.\par \par \uc2 In October, however, the high court agreed to consider whether providers could proceed with their challenge, with an expedited schedule that saw argument in November leading to last month's decision.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's conservative majority and the Fifth Circuit have described the issues with the law as procedural rather than directly related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Justice Sotomayor on Thursday called those assertions disingenuous. "The state legislature enacted a convoluted law that instills terror in those who assist women exercising their rights between 6 and 24 weeks," she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-permits-continued-enforcement-of-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11642715257"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Permits Continued Enforcement of Texas Six-Week \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ban  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220120ei1k006y1\par }\page {\par\