\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Asked to Open Way for Challenge to Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; Clinics in the state say the high court's December ruling means their lower-court suit against restrictive law should be allowed to proceed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 January 2022\par 01:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas asked the Supreme Court Monday to clear the way for the lawsuit they filed in July against the state law forbidding women from ending unwanted pregnancies after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 In legal papers, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers argued that a federal appeals court overseeing the case is obstructing their challenge with superfluous proceedings.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, known as SB 8, violates existing Supreme Court precedent that allows women to end pregnancies before fetal viability. However, the high court, by a 5-4 vote, didn't act to stop the law from going into effect on Sept. 1. It was designed to avoid federal court review by authorizing private parties to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or those who assist them instead of directing state officials to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 In October, the court agreed to decide whether the challenge to SB 8 could move forward and expedited the case. It was argued Nov. 1 and decided six weeks later, a rapid pace for the high court.\par \par \uc2 In last month's decision, by an 8-1 vote, the Supreme Court found that the Texas Medical Board and other state licensing officials play some part in SB 8's enforcement and therefore can be sued. By a 5-4 vote, the court held that other state officials, including court clerks who would docket the private lawsuits couldn't be sued in a pre-enforcement challenge.\par \par \uc2 The clinics say that Supreme Court decision requires the case be returned to federal district court in Austin for proceedings against the licensing officials; Texas says it isn't clear whether those officials play an enforcement role. At the state's request, the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals, in New Orleans, scheduled a Jan. 7 hearing on whether the Texas Supreme Court should be asked to clarify the matter.\par \par \uc2 The clinics argue that the Supreme Court had already settled that question. "Allowing the court of appeals to flout this Court's mandate and derail indefinitely the timely resolution of the merits" would render the Supreme Court's expedited consideration of the case "effectively meaningless and compound the ongoing harm to pregnant Texans under S.B. 8," the motion reads.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related cases on its docket this term. In a separate case involving Mississippi's ban on abortions after 15 weeks, the justices are weighing whether to limit or overrule Roe v. Wade. The 1973 precedent recognizes women's constitutional right to end unwanted pregnancies before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Citing "the urgency of the situation," the clinics asked the court to order the state to respond by Wednesday, allowing the justices to act ahead of Friday's Fifth Circuit hearing, should they so choose.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-asked-to-open-way-for-challenge-to-texas-abortion-law-11641258696"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Asked to Open Way for Challenge to Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220104ei140005l\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\